"""Original end-to-end designs. All numerical inputs are teaching assumptions."""
CASES={}
def case(n,raw,flow,entities,code):
    sections=[p.strip().replace(" ¶ ","\n\n") for p in raw.strip().split("\n\n")]
    assert len(sections)==22,(n,len(sections))
    CASES[n]=dict(sections=sections,flow=flow,entities=entities,code=code)

case(1, '''A user submits a long URL and receives a short code. Later, a visitor opens that code and the service redirects the visitor to the stored target. The owner can manage the link through an authenticated interface, set its expiry, and revoke it. ¶ Keep the original target and the identity of the creation request. Record clicks for analytics, but let this secondary work proceed independently: an analytics failure should not make a valid redirect wait indefinitely. Checking the target and handling abuse still belong to the main service.

For this example, aim for redirect p99 below 100 ms at the supported peak. This means that at least 99% of the measured redirects should finish within that time under the stated test conditions. A mapping reported as saved must survive the selected single-storage-node failure, and management requests must preserve ownership. ¶ We also need to say how soon revocation takes effect. If a cache keeps an old target, a fast reply may still be wrong. Choose the cache freshness rule together with the latency target and storage acknowledgment policy, then test the three together.

Assume 100 link creations/s and an average of 10,000 redirects/s. Let the redirect peak be ten times the average, or 100,000/s. Assume each mapping occupies 200 bytes before indexes and extra copies. ¶ There are 86,400 seconds in a day, so raw daily growth is 100 × 86,400 × 200 = 1,728,000,000 bytes, or 1.728 GB. For later transfer estimates, assume a 500-byte redirect response. These are teaching inputs. Real record sizes, traffic durations, and transport overhead must be measured before choosing capacity.

Begin with one web application and one relational mapping table. Give the code column a uniqueness constraint, so the database can reject a second mapping with the same code. A background job handles analytics. ¶ This arrangement makes creation and lookup easy to follow. It also exposes the first limits: the application host can fail, and all cache misses eventually reach one storage path. Measure that path before dividing the data into shards. Sharding introduces routing and uniqueness decisions, and may leave the actual bottleneck untouched.

POST /links accepts a target, expiry, and idempotency key. GET /{code} returns the permitted redirect or the documented expired or revoked result. DELETE /links/{id} checks the owner before changing state. ¶ An idempotency key identifies one intended creation. Retrying the same content with that key recovers its original result; changing the content under the same key conflicts. Validate supported URL schemes and syntax without fetching arbitrary destinations. State whether redirects are permanent or temporary, because clients may retain a permanent response after the stored link changes.

A link record contains id, unique code, target, owner_id, creation time, expiry, state, and version. An operation record binds the owner and operation key to a request fingerprint and the resulting link. Make that key unique within its owner scope. ¶ The code has a wider obligation: it must be unique throughout its intended namespace. If data is later sharded, allocation or routing must preserve this rule across owners. Separate uniqueness constraints on unrelated shards cannot do so by themselves. Expiry and revocation remain part of authoritative, versioned link state.

LinkService checks the caller and target. CodeAllocator proposes a short code. LinkRepository saves the mapping and operation result in one transaction. RedirectResolver checks whether a link is usable, while AnalyticsPublisher handles click events. ¶ The allocator may encode a suitably unique integer in base62, using 62 symbols to represent the number compactly. It may instead propose random codes and retry collisions. Either way, the repository's uniqueness decision protects the mapping. A random proposal alone proves nothing about uniqueness. Translate storage failures into the service's contract rather than exposing provider details to every caller.

Place an edge or reverse proxy before replaceable application instances. A cache can answer reads when the expiry and revocation policy permits reuse. A replicated authoritative store retains mappings; a separate queue or log carries analytics work. ¶ The cache is a copy that makes some reads cheaper. It must not be the only recoverable record of a link. If it fails, many more requests can reach storage, so bound fallback traffic. Additional application instances preserve some serving capacity; the storage placement and acknowledgment rules determine which saved mappings survive a storage failure.

On creation, validate the request, commit the mapping and deduplication result together, and only then return success. On redirect, determine whether the link is usable, obtain a cache or source value permitted by the freshness policy, and send the redirect. Record analytics independently of that required result. ¶ When many callers miss the same code, combine their source lookup where useful. Keep private management data out of the public redirect cache. These choices let the write, redirect, and analytics paths have different workloads while keeping each responsibility clear.

With a 90% cache hit ratio, 10% of redirects reach the source. At 10,000 redirects/s, that is 1,000 source reads/s; at the 100,000/s peak, it is 10,000 reads/s, before retries or combined misses. Peak response traffic is 100,000 × 500 = 50,000,000 bytes/s, or 50 MB/s, before transport overhead. ¶ A cold or unavailable cache removes the assumed protection. Test that workload separately and define how origin admission limits or rejects excess work. Success with a warm cache does not establish cold-cache capacity.

Suppose a warm redirect spends 20 ms at the edge and network, 10 ms in the application, 5 ms accessing the cache, and 20 ms returning the result. The proposed path totals 55 ms. ¶ This is a budget to test. A miss adds source lookup and possibly queueing, and individual average stage times do not establish end-to-end p99. Measure the complete distribution under hot links, cache loss, storage failover, and the revocation checks required by the contract before claiming the 100 ms objective.

Suppose two creations propose the same code. The first insert commits. The second must fail its uniqueness check without changing the first target, then try another code. If a creation commits but its reply disappears, a retry must recover the saved operation result instead of creating a new link. ¶ Other failures cross different boundaries. Cache loss can overload the source unless admission is bounded. An old cache entry can serve a revoked link unless freshness rules stop it. Analytics failure should delay analytics progress. Test each history, because a normal create-and-open demonstration does not exercise these repairs.

Add application instances when application work is the measured limit. Add shared or edge caching when repeated permitted reads justify it. Partition mappings when the authoritative storage path needs to be divided. ¶ A very popular code may still overload its one partition. More permitted read copies can help that case, whereas more partitions containing unrelated codes cannot. Every new copy inherits the expiry and revocation obligation. When data moves, routing, versions, and recovery must preserve global code ownership and the identities of existing operations.

Creating a code requires one atomic uniqueness decision: two different links cannot both win the same name. Choose an owner-facing read-after-write rule so an owner knows when the newly created mapping will appear. Public redirects may observe delayed changes only within the stated expiry and revocation contract. ¶ Click events can be aggregated later using stable event identities. Their temporary delay is different from assigning a code twice. Describe the observable rule for each operation; a single label such as “eventually consistent” would hide these differences.

Check ownership on management requests and limit abusive creation. Accept only supported target schemes. If a preview feature fetches destinations, restrict its outgoing connections and validate redirect hops; otherwise, a public URL field could be used to reach private services. ¶ Protect credentials and avoid exposing private targets in logs and analytics. A difficult-to-guess short code still grants no complete permission policy for confidential content. If the product supports restricted destinations, specify authentication and access checks for those redirects as well.

Start in one region with a stated failure contract. Use compatible rolling application releases, a suitably replicated authoritative store, and backups whose restoration has been tested. Drain connections where needed and retain operation identities when instances are replaced. ¶ New code must understand existing mapping versions. If a second region becomes necessary for locality or recovery, decide where writes are authoritative and which acknowledged changes could be lost during promotion. Geographic separation is useful only when these rules support the required recovery outcome.

Measure redirect outcomes and latency separately for hits and misses. Track origin admission, hot codes, code conflicts, storage progress, and the delay before revocation reaches permitted readers. Observe analytics lag on its own path. ¶ A high hit ratio says little about whether the cache returns a newly revoked target. Alert on useful request failures and rapid error-budget consumption. Use traces or sampled request histories to find the responsible path, while keeping private targets out of routine telemetry.

Estimate compute from instance-hours × unit rate, storage from retained bytes × copy factor × rate, and transfer from response bytes × transfer rate. State the duration used in each calculation. ¶ For example, 30 days contain 2,592,000 seconds. Sustaining 100,000 redirects/s at 500 bytes for that whole period produces 129.6 TB before overhead. A brief peak at the same rate produces much less traffic. Use observed durations and current prices for spending decisions; the example only identifies the quantities a cost model needs.

Sequentially allocated identifiers simplify code generation, provided the allocator preserves uniqueness, but they can reveal creation order. Random codes reduce that predictability and require collision checks. Neither choice supplies access control. ¶ Permanent redirects allow clients to reuse responses more aggressively, which can make later target changes or revocation harder to enforce. Temporary redirects retain more server control at a traffic cost. Compare these choices using the required mutation, revocation, privacy, and load behaviour rather than choosing a code-generation technique in isolation.

Use traffic levels as checkpoints for asking questions. Around 100 requests/s, one application and transactional store may be a useful starting point. Around 1,000, inspect query plans and indexes. Around 10,000, examine measured application and cache needs. At 100,000, expand the actual bottleneck and verify degraded and cold-cache behaviour. ¶ These numbers are not hardware promises; a particular deployment can reach its limit earlier or later. Throughout the changes, keep code and operation identities stable so that existing links and retries retain their meaning.

Three uncertainties deserve particular attention: how quickly revocation takes effect, how the source behaves after cache loss, and how code uniqueness survives partitioning. Revoke a link under load, disable or empty the cache, and force a collision across the real allocation boundary. ¶ For each check, record the permitted degraded outcome and the evidence still missing. Extra cache replicas may reduce some outages, but they do not answer all three questions. A useful review ends with a specific supported failure behaviour and a test capable of exposing a mistaken assumption.

Implement base62 encoding and a repository with an atomic unique-code constraint. Force two proposals to use the same code. Check that the first target remains unchanged and that the second creation retries safely. ¶ Next, stop execution after commit but before the creation reply. Retry with the same owner and operation key. The existing mapping should return, with no second logical creation. Change the target under that key and verify the documented conflict. This distinguishes code collisions from repeated requests: each needs its own rule.''',
['Client creates link',
 'Atomic mapping + operation commit',
 'Resolver reads cache or source',
 'Redirect; analytics deferred'],
['Owner 1 → many Links', 'Link code is UNIQUE', 'Operation key → one result', 'Analytics events are derived'],
'def allocate(target, operation_key):\n    prior = operations.find(operation_key)\n    if prior: return prior\n    while attempts_remaining():\n        code = propose_code()\n        try:\n            with transaction():\n                link = insert_unique_code(code, target)\n                save_operation_result(operation_key, link.id)\n            return link\n        except CodeConflict:\n            continue\n        except OperationConflict:\n            return load_and_validate_prior_result(operation_key, target)\n    raise AllocationUnavailable')

case(2, '''A request asks to perform work with a known cost. The limiter checks whether the caller's policy permits that work now, then allows it or supplies useful waiting or rejection guidance. ¶ Separate three requirements. A continuing rate limits sustained work; a burst allowance permits some work to arrive together; a cumulative quota limits a total over a longer period. Also state the enforcement scope. An exact global allowance and a bounded regional approximation are different policies. Giving every region a full copy of the global bucket multiplies the permitted work.

Admission should add little delay while protecting downstream resources and preserving tenant fairness. Specify what happens when the limiter cannot reach its authoritative state. ¶ During a partition, an exact global cap may require waiting or rejection because a region cannot safely know what others have spent. Continuing to allow all requests, often called failing open, cannot preserve that cap unless previously allocated safe budgets cover the work. Choose latency, availability, and precision together, with the protocol and assumptions needed to support them.

Assume 100,000 checks/s and one million active keys. Our sample bucket holds at most ten tokens and refills at two tokens/s. A token represents an amount of admitted work. ¶ At an assumed 64 bytes per key, raw state is 1,000,000 × 64 = 64 MB. Engine allocations, expiry metadata, replicas, and network work add to this. These are teaching assumptions rather than benchmarks. A small state record can still be expensive if many requests contend to update it.

Begin with a dictionary in one process. A suitable lock protects the entire refill-and-spend transition for one bucket, so two callers cannot spend its last token together. ¶ This protects only callers sharing that owner. An unrelated second process starts another allowance, and restarting may reset the first. The local design is suitable when that scope and reset behaviour meet the requirement. To enforce a shared cap across instances, we must add a shared authority or an explicit allocation protocol.

check(key,cost,policy_version) returns the decision, remaining allowance, and a meaningful retry delay. Derive the key from trusted caller and resource identity so callers cannot escape a limit by inventing new keys. Require a positive, bounded cost. ¶ Authorise policy changes and define when a new version takes effect. Distinguish overload from invalid input, and specify whether rejected attempts consume a separate abuse allowance. These meanings let callers decide whether to wait, correct the request, or stop retrying.

A bucket record contains scope_key, policy_version, tokens, last_refill, and expiry. Refill and spending belong to one atomic update. A daily cumulative quota may need separate state because it has a different lifecycle. ¶ Use integer or fixed-point units where useful to avoid numeric drift. Define how policy changes treat existing tokens and capacity. Trust the server's time basis rather than client timestamps. Expiring a record may recreate a full bucket, so expiry must be chosen as part of the protection contract.

KeyResolver establishes trusted scope, PolicyResolver loads a validated policy, and Clock provides the time basis. BucketStore owns the coordinated state transition. AdmissionService turns its result into the caller-facing decision. ¶ These boundaries make deterministic tests easier: a test can supply time and policy directly. They do not create concurrency safety by themselves. Two correct calculations can both start from one old token count. Keep load, refill, comparison, and debit under one owner or supported transaction so only one decision can spend the final token.

Use coarse admission near ingress and more specific checks before expensive resources. Independent keys can have different storage owners. A strict shared bucket still needs one atomic authority or a reviewed distributed protocol. ¶ Regions can receive bounded portions of an allowance in advance and spend locally. This reduces remote checks but introduces allocation and replenishment rules. State the outstanding-budget or overshoot bound. Protect the limiter's own capacity too: otherwise, the service intended to absorb excessive traffic may become the first overloaded dependency.

First derive trusted scope and the current policy. Under coordinated access, load the bucket and calculate elapsed time, never allowing a negative interval to add tokens. Refill by elapsed time × refill rate, capped at capacity. Compare the available tokens with cost, then debit only an allowed request. ¶ If a meaningful wait can be computed, divide the token deficit by refill rate. Record bounded metrics and return the decision. The code keeps the whole transition atomic; unprotected reads followed by writes would let two requests spend the same token.

One million 64-byte records need 64 MB of raw state. If every incoming request makes a remote check, the assumed workload also creates 100,000 limiter requests/s before retries and replica work. ¶ Measure update rate, active state, contention, and replication cost separately. Evenly distributed records do not help a single key receiving most requests: its transitions still need coordination. Batching and local budgets can reduce remote traffic, but may change decision timing or precision. Include those changes when comparing capacity.

Suppose the application has a 100 ms response target and assigns 5 ms to admission. That allocation is a proposed budget to validate under contention and failover. ¶ A strict cross-region check adds network propagation and queueing and may exceed it. Preallocated local budgets can shorten the request path, while adding replenishment work. Measure the complete path, including policy lookup and store replacement, against the required protection rule. A faster design that grants a different allowance needs to be described as a changed contract.

Imagine two requests reading one remaining token. Each sees enough allowance, and both approve the work. Combining refill, comparison, and debit atomically repairs this race because the second request then observes the first debit. ¶ Also test backward time, record expiry, forged keys, and conflicting policy versions. These can create extra allowance even when the refill formula is correct. During owner loss, apply the documented rejection, safe local budget, or bounded fail-open rule. Protection depends on identity, time, and deployment scope as well as arithmetic.

Partition independent keys while keeping each bucket's decisions coordinated. Versioned policy definitions can be cached where suitable, but token spending still needs the required authority. ¶ A strict hot bucket cannot be divided into unrelated full buckets without changing its cap. Hierarchical limits or allocated local allowances can distribute some work when their semantics fit the resource. Give expensive operations appropriate costs. Expand the part that limits protected work or coordinated checks; adding space for more cold keys will not relieve a hot decision.

A strict token bucket preserves its refill-and-debit rule under the chosen time model. A distributed approximation needs an explicit bound, such as the total allowance independently outstanding in regions. ¶ The algorithm also defines the policy: token buckets permit bursts, fixed windows have boundary effects, and sliding windows describe recent traffic differently. Usage counters that agree later cannot undo excessive work already admitted. State the invariant required when a decision is made, then choose a mechanism that preserves it.

Bind scope keys to authenticated tenant or service identity. Authorise policy administration and limit creation of new keys, since unlimited keys could exhaust state memory. Validate tenant headers, policy names, and time inputs. ¶ Limit what remaining-allowance responses reveal about other callers. Admission and permission answer different questions: having enough tokens does not permit reading another tenant's document. The protected operation must still enforce its resource access rule.

Deploy policy and bucket owners with stated state-loss and failover behaviour. If replacement grants every caller a new full bucket, the system may admit another burst that the contract did not allow. ¶ Roll out policy versions compatibly and test older callers, clock behaviour, and owner failure. A fallback must have an acceptable bound. Preserve state or allocate safe replacement allowance according to the design, even though each individual bucket record is small.

Measure allowed and rejected cost, check latency, owner errors, active keys, hot-key pressure, and policy-version disagreement. Compare these signals with the load and health of the protected resource. ¶ Many rejections can mean successful protection or a mistaken policy denying legitimate work. Few rejections can hide fail-open behaviour during state-store failure. Observe both unsafe admission and unnecessary denial. Keep metric dimensions bounded so one label per arbitrary key does not overwhelm monitoring.

Cost includes state memory, check requests, replicated updates, transfer, and operating work. A remote strict check on every inexpensive business operation can cost more than the work it protects. ¶ Compare strict and local-budget designs with their guarantees stated, using symbolic or explicitly fictional rates for teaching. If a cheaper design allows more overshoot or behaves differently during failure, include that consequence. A smaller infrastructure bill alone does not establish equal protection.

A token bucket admits bounded bursts. A leaky-bucket shaper smooths output by making work wait. Fixed-window counters are simple, but traffic can cluster on both sides of a window boundary. Sliding approaches track recent traffic at additional state or computation cost. ¶ Regional budgets add allocation work in exchange for shorter local decisions and some independence during failure. Choose using the protected resource's needs. Include the rejection response and expected retry behaviour, because callers can otherwise recreate the same burst immediately.

First build a deterministic local bucket with one atomic owner. Introduce shared state when multiple processes need one cap. Partition keys when measured load requires it. ¶ Add regional allocation for a specific latency or failure requirement and state its bound. At every step, preserve the original policy meaning or explicitly version the changed contract. Increasing the number of enforcement instances should not silently increase the customer's allowance.

Review whether the advertised scope matches actual enforcement. Ask what a policy update does to existing tokens and how the hottest key behaves. These questions cannot be answered by the refill formula alone. ¶ Test simultaneous spending of the last token, backward time, expiry, policy replacement, owner loss, and regional reconnection. Full independent buckets do not support an exact-global claim. Record unresolved evidence about the real protocol rather than assuming that a shared storage product automatically establishes every required behaviour.

Build the sample bucket with an injected monotonic clock. Spend its ten initial tokens, reject an immediate eleventh one-token spend, then advance three seconds. At two tokens/s, six tokens should return, within capacity ten. ¶ Reject invalid costs and check that backward time adds no allowance. Reproduce the race in which two unprotected readers spend one token, then coordinate the transition and confirm that only one wins. Keep both histories so the test explains why the repair works.''',
['Trusted request key and cost',
 'Atomic refill and debit',
 'Allow or reject under policy',
 'Observe protection of downstream'],
['Policy version defines bucket',
 'Scope key owns one state',
 'Region budget has explicit bound',
 'Quota is a separate lifecycle'],
'def spend(bucket, now, cost):\n    require(cost > 0)\n    elapsed = max(0, now - bucket.last)\n    available = min(bucket.capacity,\n                    bucket.tokens + elapsed * bucket.rate)\n    allowed = available >= cost\n    bucket.tokens = available - cost if allowed else available\n    bucket.last = max(bucket.last, now)\n    return allowed\n# Entire transition executes atomically for this bucket.')

case(3, '''Two people exchange messages in an authorised conversation. Online devices receive updates; disconnected devices later fetch missing history. Support bounded groups and a defined retention policy as well. ¶ A message moves through several stages: the service accepts it, storage persists it, a device receives it, and a person may read it. Give each receipt the meaning its evidence supports. Presence is an approximate indication of connectivity. A WebSocket supplies a live communication path, but opening it proves neither durable history nor human reading.

Retain acknowledged messages under the selected storage-failure contract and protect conversation privacy. Define ordering within each conversation, or state a deliberately weaker rule. For the supported workload, target online delivery below one second. ¶ An offline device has a different delivery timeline and catches up later. Approximate presence expiry need not satisfy the strict rules used for exclusive allocation. Specify retention and visibility for moderation, deletion, and returning devices so recovery does not reveal history they may no longer access.

Assume 100,000 concurrent connections and 10,000 accepted messages/s. Each stored message averages 500 bytes and produces five recipient deliveries in this simplified workload. ¶ Ingestion is 10,000 × 500 = 5 MB/s. Over 86,400 seconds, that is 432 GB/day. Five deliveries multiply body traffic to about 25 MB/s before framing and overhead. Model multiple devices, large groups, connection memory, and reconnect bursts separately. These assumptions describe work to test; they do not establish gateway or database capacity.

Begin with one connection server and a transactional history store. Commit the message before returning a persistence receipt, then try to deliver it to online devices. ¶ If the gateway crashes, clients lose their connections, but committed history can survive under the storage contract. They reconnect and fetch missing messages. This separates the server's availability limit from message persistence. A database commit says the message was retained; only later evidence can establish delivery to a particular device.

A send includes conversation_id, client_message_id, and bounded content. Fetch history after a sequence with an operation such as GET /conversations/{id}/messages?after=sequence. Check membership on both reads and writes. ¶ Delivered and read receipts identify the relevant device or user and the highest sequence covered by the product's policy. A repeated client_message_id in the sender's uniqueness scope returns the original message. A client can therefore recover from a lost send reply without adding another logical message.

Store conversations and membership separately from messages. Each message contains conversation_id, sequence, sender, client_message_id, body, and version. Receipt records hold delivered and read progress for the appropriate user or device; heartbeat records provide expiring presence. ¶ Enforce the intended client-identity uniqueness scope. A sequence authority, or a supported conflict model, must establish conversation order. Client-clock timestamps alone cannot supply a stable total order. Keep the identity used for retries valid across reconnects.

ConnectionGateway owns live connections. MembershipPolicy checks access. MessageService coordinates identity and persistence, SequenceOwner assigns conversation order, and HistoryRepository reads bounded pages. DeliveryWorker distributes committed messages. ¶ ReceiptAggregator combines progress without moving backward under the chosen device policy. Large groups need bounded fan-out work rather than one long loop inside the message transaction. These components can initially run together, but their contracts should distinguish durable history from routing information that may disappear during a gateway restart.

Use replaceable connection gateways and route durable message work by conversation. Commit delivery intent through a log or outbox, then let workers find currently connected recipient devices. An outbox is a durable record of work to publish, saved with the message so a crash cannot lose the obligation between the two writes. ¶ Connection routes may be stale. A missed frame is therefore repaired from history. If the broker is unavailable, committed intent can wait while acknowledged history remains retained. The gateway is a route to the device, rather than the only record of its messages.

The client sends a stable message identity. The service checks membership, assigns a sequence, and commits both message and delivery intent before returning the persistence result. The code illustrates this durable boundary; device delivery and read receipts follow later. ¶ Workers send frames and apply valid receipts. On reconnect, a device supplies its last observed sequence, fetches bounded missing pages, and detects gaps. It recognises repeated frames by identity. This lets the retained conversation and the device's current display recover independently after an interrupted live connection.

The assumptions give 5 MB/s of raw storage growth, or 432 GB/day. Five recipients per message produce 50,000 deliveries/s and 25 MB/s of message bodies. ¶ Measure connection memory separately. At an illustrative 20 kB each, 100,000 connections need 2 GB before other process memory or queued content. Bound outbound buffers. A slow device may receive less than a sender produces, allowing its queue to grow until the gateway runs out of memory even though storage remains healthy.

Suppose ingress takes 20 ms, commit coordination 30 ms, delivery queues 20 ms, and the recipient path 80 ms. The budget totals 150 ms before rendering. ¶ This does not establish online p99. Test hot conversations, congestion, failover, and slow-device buffers. Measure persistence-receipt delay, device-delivery delay, and human-read delay separately. Offline delivery follows a different timeline, and a successful transport reply cannot establish every later stage.

If the send reply disappears after commit, the repeated client identity recovers the saved message. If a gateway stops, devices reconnect and catch up from history. If a device consumes messages too slowly, disconnect or reduce its service before its buffer exhausts the gateway. ¶ Membership removal can race with cached access state, so enforce the documented visibility rule on live delivery and history. Reconnect storms need admission controls and staggered retries. Test these event sequences directly; two permanently connected clients do not exercise them.

Distribute independent gateway connections and partition history and ordering by conversation where useful. Let bounded workers perform group fan-out. ¶ One popular conversation can still concentrate ordering and delivery work. It may require explicitly different large-channel semantics instead of including every recipient in a low-latency acknowledgment chain. Preserve sequences and deduplication identities when ownership moves. Include history catch-up in capacity tests, because many devices returning after a gateway failure produce a different workload from ordinary message averages.

Define a per-conversation order, and make the sender's confirmation agree with the saved message. A history cursor must resume without silently skipping required records. Cross-conversation total order is unnecessary unless the product requires it. ¶ Live frames may repeat, so devices deduplicate by identity. Presence and receipts may propagate later, while delivered and read progress follow the chosen monotonic model: old updates must not move progress backward. These are separate guarantees; using WebSockets does not by itself provide exactly-once messaging.

Enforce membership on sends, history, fan-out, search, and media access. Limit abusive sending, payload size, and group size. Keep message bodies out of routine telemetry. ¶ If end-to-end encryption is required, specify device keys, recovery, membership changes, and the resulting limits on server features. TLS protects a connection but does not supply that full product guarantee. Attachments also need access and retention rules. An authorised chat page does not automatically protect every derived content URL.

Drain gateways during rollouts and support reconnects from older clients. Evolve message and receipt formats compatibly. Preserve history through the chosen replication and promotion rules. ¶ Test reconnect and catch-up alongside ordinary sending. Restarting every gateway together may create a much larger burst than normal traffic. A new region needs explicit ordering and membership behaviour. Retain client identities and progress during replacement so uncertain sends do not become duplicated history records.

Measure persistence delay, live-delivery lag, sequence-gap recovery, reconnect rates, fan-out backlog, and hot conversations. Observe slow-device limits and membership denials too. ¶ Correlate client and server identities in suitable detailed records without adding one unbounded metric label per message. Synthetic clients can send, disconnect, reconnect, and check both history and forbidden access. This follows the user's journey. A healthy connection count alone cannot establish that accepted messages are retained or revoked devices are blocked.

Separate costs for connections, retained history, fan-out transfer, indexes, replicas, media, and telemetry. Thirty days of the assumed raw history need 432 GB/day × 30 = 12.96 TB before indexes and copies. ¶ Media can dominate storage and transfer, so give it a separate quota and lifecycle. Use explicit or symbolic rates rather than assumed provider prices. Cost per retained message describes a different burden from cost per delivered body, especially when recipients and devices multiply the delivery path.

WebSockets support bidirectional communication with low live delay, while requiring connection management. Server-Sent Events (SSE) can carry server updates alongside HTTP sends for suitable clients. Polling is simpler but creates requests during idle periods. ¶ A single conversation sequence authority simplifies order and creates a hot-key boundary. Concurrent regional writing needs an explicit merge and ordering model and changes the contract. Compare alternatives using offline recovery, receipts, and privacy; transport features alone do not decide the architecture.

First make durable history and reconnect recovery correct. Add gateway replicas with explicit connection routing. Introduce durable fan-out for growing volume or groups, then partition conversation ownership when measured storage or ordering work requires it. ¶ Regional placement introduces another authority and failure decision. Throughout these changes, retain message identity: a retry after a shard move or gateway replacement should still name the same logical send. Increasing connection capacity while losing that identity can create duplicate messages.

Review the meanings of “sent” and “delivered,” the hottest conversation, slow-device memory, and membership revocation. For each, describe an event sequence and its required result. ¶ Test a commit followed by a lost reply, a dropped frame, gateway loss, stale routing, and a history request after membership removal. Check both the resulting list and receipts. These checks examine recovery and privacy, which a demonstration with two always-online clients cannot establish.

Build a client model that recognises message identities and detects sequence gaps. Save a message but suppress its live frame. Reconnect from an earlier sequence and confirm that history adds the message once to the visible list. ¶ Then repeat its client_message_id and verify that storage creates no second logical message. Deduplicating displayed frames and deduplicating history creation protect different boundaries. Test both so a neat display does not hide duplicated durable records.''',
['Client sends stable message ID',
 'Conversation owner commits sequence',
 'Delivery fans out to devices',
 'Reconnect reads durable history'],
['Conversation 1 → many Messages',
 'Membership authorizes participant',
 'Message has client dedup identity',
 'Receipt tracks monotonic progress'],
'with transaction():\n    existing = find_by_client_id(sender, client_message_id)\n    if existing: return existing\n    require_member(sender, conversation)\n    seq = allocate_conversation_sequence(conversation)\n    msg = insert_message(conversation, seq, body)\n    insert_outbox(msg.id, "deliver")\nreturn persisted_receipt(msg)\n# Device delivery and read receipt happen later.')

case(4, '''An application records an intent to notify someone. The platform chooses permitted email, SMS, or push channels, renders a versioned template, submits the message, and tracks the outcome. Some intents may be scheduled or cancelled within a defined window. ¶ Keep message purpose in the policy: operational and product messages may follow different preference rules. A channel adapter handles delivery mechanics; it should not silently decide which messages the user may receive. Provider acceptance, device delivery, and human viewing need distinct states and evidence.

Preserve accepted intents and make delay, expiry, and permanent failure visible. Give each message a business expiry. A password-reset message delivered hours late may be useless or harmful even if submission eventually succeeds. ¶ Reserve resources for critical work so a marketing batch cannot occupy every worker. Avoid duplicate external effects where the provider contract supports it and expose uncertainty where it does not. The promised outcome must match what the service can establish.

Assume ten million intents/day. Dividing by 86,400 seconds gives about 115.7 intents/s; a tenfold peak gives about 1,157/s. At 1 kB of initial intent and attempt metadata each, raw growth is 10 GB/day. ¶ Retries, retained content, indexes, and copies add work. Treat channel quotas as separate configured inputs to check with the actual provider. The daily service average does not establish how quickly one SMS or email route can submit its share.

A first version calls a provider directly after a user action. The user waits for it, and a crash after the business commit but before sending can lose the notification obligation. ¶ Save required intent in the business transaction or its outbox, then let a worker submit it. This closes that local loss window and lets a provider outage delay delivery without erasing accepted work. The repair introduces queued state, attempts, and recovery ownership; an untracked background function would not supply these guarantees.

POST /notifications accepts recipient, template version, variables, channel policy, expiry, and idempotency key. GET /notifications/{id} reports pending, submitted, delivered, expired, failed, or cancelled according to defined transitions. ¶ Cancellation cannot recall a message already accepted by a provider. Callback endpoints verify the sender and recognise repeated events. A delivered state requires the relevant provider evidence, rather than simply an exception-free worker call. State which later outcomes remain outside the platform's knowledge.

Store the intent, template version, relevant preference information, channel/recipient tasks, attempts, and a provider-event inbox. Stable identities and uniqueness rules bind each intended effect to its task. Retain provider references, including uncertain outcomes. ¶ Separate sensitive content from minimal progress and audit records. Decide whether a preference change affects already queued work. Where the policy requires current consent or cancellation checks, a submission-time snapshot alone is insufficient.

NotificationService records intent, PreferencePolicy selects allowed channels, and TemplateRenderer creates content. SenderStrategy selects channel behaviour; ProviderAdapter translates that provider's protocol and outcomes. DeliveryRepository owns attempts and durable progress. ¶ Factories can construct genuine channel variations, and decorators can measure calls, provided their lifetimes and result meanings stay clear. A retry wrapper must preserve operation identity and handle an uncertain prior submission. Sharing a method signature does not make two providers' recovery contracts interchangeable.

Use durable ingress, preference and template access, channel queues, bounded workers, callback inboxes, and an outcome store. Separate critical and bulk capacity where their priorities require it. ¶ Limit how much capacity one failing provider can occupy. For recovery, intent, attempt state, queue acknowledgment, and progress must agree about ownership. Draw those durable boundaries with the normal send path so a worker or broker restart has a clear starting point.

Deduplicate and save the intent, choose permitted content and channel, then persist a delivery task. Before submitting, the worker checks current attempt authority, expiry, and the required current-preference policy. ¶ Use a stable provider operation identity where supported. Save either the submission evidence or an uncertain outcome before acknowledging according to the task protocol. Verified callbacks may arrive before or after the attempt reply. Apply them by identity and allowed transition, so an old event cannot incorrectly reverse later progress.

The overall average is about 115.7 intents/s, but channels have separate limits. Suppose SMS tasks arrive at 100/s while the route submits 40/s. The queue gains 100 − 40 = 60 tasks/s. If both rates persist for ten minutes, 60 × 600 = 36,000 submissions accumulate. ¶ Include retries, recipient fan-out, and expiry. Some waiting messages may become useless before the queue drains. Admission, priority, or a supported alternate route must respond to that channel limit, regardless of the service-wide average.

Suppose durable API acceptance takes 50 ms, queue wait takes 2 seconds, and provider submission takes 500 ms. Provider acceptance then occurs around 2.55 seconds after the API request. ¶ These are illustrative budgets, not measured percentiles. Device delivery and human viewing require further events and evidence. Measure the end event that matters for the message's purpose as well as API latency. A quick acknowledgment only establishes quick acceptance of the obligation.

A worker submits a message and dies before recording the reply. The provider may already have accepted it. Recover using a supported status query or provider idempotency; if neither resolves the outcome, retain the duplicate risk explicitly. ¶ Switching providers can create a second message while the first is still in flight. Also handle malformed templates, bounded outage retries, expiry, and forged callbacks. Reconcile old submitted or uncertain tasks rather than leaving their outcome permanently implied by an empty worker queue.

Distribute independent recipients and channel tasks, preserving any required per-recipient order and concurrency bounds. More workers help only while the downstream route has capacity. ¶ Add providers with explicit selection, quota, and uncertainty rules. An alternate route may improve some outages, but timeout followed by switching can submit twice. Keep critical capacity and business expiry visible. Useful, permitted progress is a better scaling objective than the largest possible number of parallel submissions.

Commit accepted intent and its deduplication result atomically. Task delivery may repeat, so local transitions recognise stable identities. Provider acceptance and final delivery each require their own evidence. ¶ Retain the template version so a retry does not change content under an old identity, and apply current preferences at the boundary the policy requires. Email, SMS, and push contracts differ. Name the local record or external effect protected by each mechanism instead of assigning a general exactly-once guarantee.

Authorise which actors may send particular templates to particular recipients. Validate variables and encode or escape them for their channel, including safe header and content construction. Protect recipient data, credentials, and callback authenticity. ¶ Bound intent creation to prevent abuse. Keep message bodies and tokens out of routine logs. Links inside messages need an appropriate audience and lifetime; rendering or delivery must not accidentally broaden the access they grant.

Deploy workers with durable attempt state and graceful draining so scale-down does not abandon unrecorded work. Template changes create new versions rather than altering content behind an existing retry identity. ¶ Evolve task schemas compatibly and plan credential rotation. Test replacement during an outage and recovery: accumulated work must not flood a returning provider. Verify that acceptance and critical delivery still work when another channel is saturated.

Track accepted intents, queue age by channel, expiry, submission delay, uncertain outcomes, callbacks, and duplicate recognition. Separate critical and bulk outcomes so a good aggregate does not hide delayed important messages. ¶ Alert on old unresolved tasks and rapid error-budget consumption. A queue can age into an incident without any worker exception. Correlate task, attempt, and provider evidence without exposing private content. Enqueue success is one observable stage of the journey.

Include intent and attempt storage, provider-submitted units, workers, callbacks, transfer, and operating effort. A retry can be billable even when it produces no useful delivery. Model channel_unit_price × submitted_units using explicit inputs. ¶ Content length and character encoding may affect SMS billing under the selected provider's contract, which must be checked. Cost per accepted intent and cost per relevant successful delivery describe different work; keep their denominators separate.

Direct synchronous sending uses little infrastructure and ties the user's request to the provider. Queued delivery adds durable state, recovery, and delay. An orchestrator can make escalation across channels explicit, while independent event reactions can make overall progress harder to inspect. ¶ A backup provider adds both a recovery option and an uncertainty boundary. Compare alternatives using expiry, criticality, permissions, and available evidence. Eventually submitting something may fail the original notification purpose.

Start with one sender behind a clear contract. Add durable intent and a worker when required work must survive failure or leave the user's request path. Introduce channel strategies and factories for real variation. ¶ Separate critical and bulk resources as volume and harm justify it. Add failover and escalation after defining uncertain-outcome recovery. Preserve task, template, and attempt identities through these changes so scaling does not accidentally create fresh effects for the same intent.

Review whether “delivered” matches the evidence, whether expiry and current preferences are checked, and whether failover can duplicate a message. These questions involve product meaning as well as implementation. ¶ Test send-then-crash, forged and repeated callbacks, preference revocation after enqueue, template changes, and channel saturation. Late events must not incorrectly reverse terminal progress. Successful submission requests alone cannot establish timely delivery to the intended people.

Build a recording sender that stores a submission but returns uncertainty. Process the task again and inspect its attempt identities and local state. It must not invent delivery evidence. ¶ Add expiry and current-preference checks. Then switch providers after the uncertain submission and demonstrate how a second external effect can result. Implement only the recovery your mock provider supports, and make the remaining duplicate risk visible.''',
['Durable notification intent',
 'Preference and template policy',
 'Bounded channel submission',
 'Verified callback or reconciliation'],
['Intent owns delivery tasks',
 'Task has versioned template',
 'Attempt has provider reference',
 'Callback inbox deduplicates events'],
'def deliver(task, now):\n    require_current_attempt(task)\n    if now >= task.expires_at: return mark_expired(task)\n    if not permitted_now(task): return mark_cancelled(task)\n    sender = sender_factory.for_channel(task.channel)\n    result = sender.send(task.stable_provider_key, render(task))\n    if result.uncertain:\n        return persist_uncertain(task, result.reference)\n    return persist_submission_result(task, result)\n# A provider acknowledgment is not a human-read receipt.')

case(5, '''Users publish posts, follow accounts, and read a ranked home feed. Provide bounded continuation while enforcing privacy, blocking, moderation, and deletion. ¶ Ranking chooses among permitted posts. A high score cannot grant visibility. Candidate lists and engagement totals can lag their source, but the product must define their acceptable freshness. Separate finding interesting posts from deciding which posts the viewer may receive.

Assume a feed p99 target of 300 ms at the supported workload. Retain accepted posts, define how promptly access changes take effect, and give pagination understandable duplicate and omission behaviour. ¶ Ranking may change between refreshes. State whether a refresh begins a new generation and what its continuation cursor preserves. Because candidate caches can be old, specify which source and permission properties are rechecked on every served page.

Assume ten million daily readers make twenty feed reads each. That is 200 million reads/day, or about 2,315/s. Assume one million posts/day. ¶ For ordinary authors, use a simplified average of one hundred followers and a 32-byte candidate reference. Preparing all those references creates 100 million/day, or 3.2 GB raw. A celebrity with millions of followers belongs to a different workload class: this average hides both the publication burst and work spent for inactive followers.

Start with indexed posts and follow relationships. When a reader requests a feed, retrieve posts by followed authors and rank the candidates. This avoids maintaining a prepared copy for every follower. ¶ As follow sets grow, retrieval and ranking can make each read expensive. Measure that work and the follower distribution before materialising feeds. Materialisation means storing a derived result in advance; it saves repeated reads while adding publication work, storage, and refresh responsibilities.

POST /posts creates a versioned post. POST /follows changes a relationship. GET /feed returns a bounded page, continuation cursor, and relevant ranking generation. DELETE /posts/{id} records authoritative removal. ¶ Bind cursors to the viewer and applicable filters, validate them, and define their snapshot or continuation meaning. A cursor locates a page; it does not preserve permission after access changes. Reapply the required visibility policy whenever another page is served.

Posts contain identity, author, version, visibility, state, and creation time. Follows connect reader and author. Optional candidate records connect reader to post and source version, with ranking information. Retain model version or generation where reproducibility needs it. ¶ Posts and access relationships are authoritative; candidates are derived references. Stable source identity supports deduplication, removal, and rebuilds. State whether copied features are snapshots or are expected to follow later updates.

PostService owns publication and FollowService owns relationship changes. CandidateGenerator finds or prepares references, FeatureFetcher obtains bounded ranking inputs, and Ranker scores them. VisibilityFilter enforces access; FeedAssembler merges, deduplicates, and pages results. ¶ Keep visibility independent of desirability. Missing features may change ranking, but must not broaden permission. These boundaries let us improve candidate or feature performance without moving access rules into a model score.

Commit publications durably with outbox intent. Workers prepare candidates for ordinary authors, while high-fan-out authors can be retrieved at read time. Candidate storage, ranking compute, and permitted caches support serving. ¶ This hybrid avoids immediately writing every celebrity post into every follower's feed. It introduces a rule for choosing and changing strategies, plus reconciliation work. Keep model deployment decisions distinct from the data path, and preserve source state and access checks whenever derived copies lag.

Publication commits the post and fan-out intent. Workers insert candidate references under stable identity. A read combines prepared candidates with posts pulled from high-fan-out authors, checks required current access and post state, fetches bounded features, ranks, deduplicates, and returns a continuation. ¶ Track the generation. A deleted reference may remain physically stored while serving rejects it, if the deletion contract permits that delay. Permission to expose a post and eventual storage cleanup have separate completion boundaries.

The assumed 200 million daily reads average about 2,315/s; a fivefold peak is about 11,574/s. One million posts × one hundred followers creates 100 million candidate writes/day. ¶ A post reaching ten million followers creates its own burst, regardless of the average. Also estimate candidates ranked per request and feature lookups per candidate. A modest API rate can multiply into much larger storage and compute demand through these internal fan-outs.

Suppose the path spends 20 ms at the edge, 50 ms retrieving candidates, 80 ms fetching features, 70 ms ranking, and 30 ms preparing a response. The proposed total is 250 ms before further tail and queue allowance. ¶ Measure end-to-end p99 against the 300 ms objective. One required slow feature can dominate completion, so define safe missing-feature behaviour. Measure publication freshness separately: a fast page can still contain old or empty candidates.

A worker may repeat fan-out after a crash. Stable candidate identity prevents the repeat from adding duplicate effects. A celebrity burst needs its selected pull or bounded distribution path to avoid unrestricted queue growth. ¶ Privacy changes can precede candidate cleanup, so current serving checks remain essential. If ranking fails, a permitted chronological feed may be an acceptable fallback. Test access on that fallback too; its simpler ordering does not remove deletion or visibility obligations.

Partition candidate work by a measured ownership key, batch feature reads, and cache within the allowed freshness scope. Bound candidates retrieved and ranked per read. Give rebuilds separate budgets so they cannot consume all live-serving resources. ¶ Choose the push/pull threshold from write amplification, reader activity, and read cost. Moving an author across that threshold needs a transition that prevents missing and duplicate candidates. More workers help only while the stores they update have remaining capacity.

Post identity, updates, and deletion need a suitable transactional authority. Candidate projections and engagement totals may propagate later. An author who expects an immediate view of their own post may need a direct-source overlay or another supported read path. ¶ Access still follows its own freshness rule even when candidates are stale. Specify which page properties belong to a ranking snapshot and which are revalidated. The feed contains several observable consistency requirements.

Enforce author permissions, viewer visibility, blocking, and moderation on every relevant path. Validate cursors and bound scraping and feature-query amplification. Client scores must never grant access. ¶ Ranking records may expose private behaviour. Limit retention and access, and propagate required deletion into features and analytical copies. Apply tenant and content-scope rules to model inputs and outputs as well as the final page.

Version rankers, use representative canary cohorts, and check rollback compatibility with stored features. Mixed-version readers must understand fan-out formats during rollout. ¶ Separate rebuild capacity from live reads and publication. A region-local feed needs a defined route for source and access-policy updates before promising privacy and local reads together. Observe model releases alongside code and schema releases because each can change the served result.

Track publication-to-candidate delay, candidate counts, visibility denials, read latency, feature tails, model versions, duplicates, and hot-author backlog. Measure relevance and useful content separately from infrastructure timing. ¶ Low p99 does not establish correct, varied, current pages. Pair operational measures with content and privacy checks. Detailed evidence can retain source versions and generations without adding unbounded per-user or per-post labels to metrics.

Include candidate writes, feature storage and reads, ranking CPU, response transfer, and rebuilds. The simplified 3.2 GB/day of raw references excludes indexes and other metadata. ¶ Compare push and pull using the fraction of followers who actually read. Preparing feeds for inactive readers can spend work never used. Use explicit fictional rates or verified current quotes, and compare designs with equal visibility, retention, freshness, and recovery requirements.

Fan-out-on-write performs work when a post arrives to make later reads cheaper. Fan-out-on-read avoids unused preparation and adds work to each active read. A hybrid assigns different traffic classes to each and adds transition complexity. ¶ Precomputed ranking can save serving CPU while adding freshness delay. Compare these choices with active-reader ratios and author skew. Include deletion and privacy requirements in the comparison, since a faster page with weaker visibility is a different service contract.

Begin with indexed pull reads, then add bounded ranking and useful caching. Prepare ordinary-author candidates when repeated read work becomes limiting. Introduce a distinct high-fan-out path after measuring author skew. ¶ Regional copies need source-version and privacy-update rules. Revisit thresholds as reading, posting, and follow distributions change. Preserve stable candidate identity and explicit generations so each stage retains the intended continuation and duplicate behaviour.

Review inactive-reader assumptions, high-degree authors, and access revocation. Put a post into cached candidate pages, then delete it or make it private. Verify that later pages obey the visibility rule. ¶ Replay fan-out, pause a worker, and disable a feature dependency. Check candidate identities, source versions, and fallback access. An empty queue establishes progress through a worker, but does not prove that each intended reader obtained one usable, authorised reference.

Build push and pull generation over a small follow graph containing one very high-degree author. Count work for explicit user journeys so inactive-reader costs remain visible. ¶ Delete a materialised post and test the serving filter. Replay a fan-out event and check for visible duplicates. Explain which strategy spends work before anyone reads and which moves work into active reads, while enforcing the same privacy rules in both.''',
['Post and outbox commit',
 'Push ordinary; pull hot authors',
 'Merge authorized candidates',
 'Rank, paginate and revalidate'],
['Author owns many Posts',
 'Follow maps reader to author',
 'Candidate references source version',
 'Generation identifies ranked page'],
'def feed(reader, cursor):\n    candidates = materialized(reader) + pull_hot_authors(reader)\n    unique = deduplicate_by_post_id(candidates)\n    permitted = [p for p in unique if visible_now(reader, p)]\n    features = fetch_bounded_features(permitted)\n    ranked = rank(permitted, features, model_version)\n    return page(ranked, validated_cursor(cursor, reader))')

case(6, '''An owner uploads a video, the service validates and converts it, and an authorised viewer receives a playback manifest and media segments. Support resumable upload, processing progress, and deletion. ¶ Adaptive playback lets a client switch among available renditions as conditions change. A rendition is an encoded version suitable for a particular playback profile. Switching cannot repair missing media. We need both a transformation workflow and a deliberate decision that a complete generation is ready to play.

Preserve sources and required derived media under stated durability and retention rules. Choose playback-start and smoothness objectives for supported clients and measure the delivery journey. ¶ Asynchronous processing must expose failed and review states. Protect manifests, segments, thumbnails, and download links as well as the player page. A private metadata row cannot protect media bytes that remain publicly fetchable.

Assume 1,000 uploads/day averaging 1 GB and thirty minutes each. That is 1 TB/day of source bytes and 500 source-hours/day. If selected renditions total five output GB per source GB, they add 5 TB/day. ¶ Assume 10,000 simultaneous viewers average 3 Mb/s each. Delivery is 30 Gb/s; dividing by eight gives 3.75 GB/s before overhead. Encoding, retention, and playback impose different work. These teaching ratios are not codec or infrastructure benchmarks.

A prototype accepts an upload and lets one worker create one playback file. This is easy to trace, but large transfers and computation depend on fragile local execution, and one file adapts poorly to varying networks. ¶ Direct upload to storage keeps large bytes out of application workers. Durable encoding tasks retain required progress. Start with a small supported rendition set; producing many unused formats adds compute and storage without necessarily improving the intended viewers' experience.

POST /videos creates an upload intent. Scoped multipart authorisation permits transfer; completion validates the stored input. A status endpoint reports progress, and playback returns an authorised manifest reference. Deletion tracks required cleanup. ¶ Distinguish upload complete, processing, playable, and failed. Define playback-capability scope, lifetime, and cache behaviour so an old URL does not grant indefinite access. Accepted bytes are input to processing, rather than proof of usable media.

Store video identity, owner, source version, and state. Upload sessions track unfinished transfers. Encoding jobs reference input digest, profile version, and attempt epoch. Renditions identify profile, version, object key, and checksum. A manifest generation names its required artifacts. ¶ Publish only after verifying that set. Keep published references immutable, introducing a new version when bytes change. Source and owner relationships let deletion, repair, and inventory reconciliation find every derived object.

UploadService grants upload permission, MediaValidator checks supported bounded input, and EncodingPlanner chooses profiles. WorkerLease manages attempts; TranscoderAdapter performs isolated conversion. ArtifactPublisher advances published metadata only after required outputs are verified. ¶ PlaybackService checks access and issues scoped references. Separate writing artifacts from publishing a generation: finishing one file says nothing about the remaining required files. Publication needs evidence about the whole set.

Use object storage for immutable sources and segments, durable workflow state and a task queue, isolated transcoders, metadata storage, and edge delivery. The application handles control and access while bytes flow through storage and CDN facilities. ¶ Give untrusted parsers limited resources, permissions, and egress. Keep source and derived retention explicit. The queue schedules work; source storage, workflow state, and published metadata still have distinct recovery responsibilities.

Validate the completed upload's identity and choose a versioned plan. An attempt produces artifacts, then verifies the required segments and manifest. Atomically publish the playable metadata generation only when that set is valid. ¶ Failed attempts may leave unpublished objects for cleanup or safe reuse. Viewers obtain authorised references and fetch supported renditions. Publishing after only the first output is valid only under an explicitly supported progressive-publication contract. Otherwise, it exposes an incomplete generation as ready.

Thirty days of the assumed input need 30 TB of originals and 150 TB of renditions before copies and overhead. Viewer transfer at 30 Gb/s is much larger than metadata traffic. ¶ Encoding capacity depends on measured content and profile costs. For illustration, two compute-hours per source-hour would turn 500 source-hours/day into 1,000 compute-hours/day. Measure that ratio with representative media before sizing workers; the calculation provides a workload assumption, not a performance result.

Suppose playback authorisation takes 50 ms, manifest delivery 30 ms, and initial buffering 800 ms. The example path totals 880 ms. ¶ A cache miss or constrained client changes this path. Measure startup, rebuffering, rendition switches, and failures separately from metadata API p99. Segment delivery and player behaviour are part of the experience. A quick permission check provides little evidence about a missing or slowly arriving first segment.

An interrupted upload, corrupt source, partial encoding, and missing published object are different failures. Give each an explicit state and repair path. Keep partial generations unpublished and verify the final set. ¶ If edge delivery fails, fallback can send enormous traffic to the origin, so protect it. Revocation and deletion must affect playable references and copies under the selected capability and cache rules. Test through actual media requests rather than checking only metadata state.

Scale upload transfer, encoding, and playback separately. Bound encoding concurrency and prioritise jobs by deadlines. Cache immutable segments where access policy permits reuse. ¶ A viral video creates hot-object delivery pressure, which may leave metadata storage mostly unaffected. Plan origin protection and regional-copy cost during cache loss. More profiles and transcoders also create backfill and storage work. Expand the measured limiting path rather than increasing every component together.

A published generation must name a usable artifact set. Attempts may repeat, but stable output identities and conditional completion prevent an old worker from replacing a newer generation. An epoch is an attempt-authority number; publication rechecks it at the authoritative transition, as the code illustrates. ¶ Declare cache reuse intervals and separate privacy and deletion requirements. A recovery region must possess the objects required by its publication metadata. Copying only some bytes does not make a complete failover source.

Authorise upload and playback, bound sizes and supported formats, isolate parsers and encoders, and give workers narrow storage permissions. Protect signed capabilities and keep them out of routine logs. ¶ Check manifests, segments, and thumbnails directly. Test capability expiry, cache keys, membership removal, and deletion end to end. Privacy must cover every route to content, including objects fetched without reopening the player page.

Version encoding profiles and retain usable older outputs until a validated transition permits removal. Workers need durable claims and graceful shutdown. Give expensive backfills budgets that preserve live processing and playback. ¶ Test restoring metadata to an older point while source bytes survive. Source identities and artifact inventories must let the application reconcile what exists. Preserving running containers alone cannot repair a lost relationship between media and metadata.

Observe upload completion, oldest jobs, failed profiles, compute-to-media ratios, and ready-generation integrity. Track edge hits, origin transfer, startup, rebuffering, and access outcomes for playback. ¶ Correlate source, profile, and generation in detailed evidence. Alert on missing segments and stuck videos even when workers report few exceptions. A synthetic private-video journey should include successful playback followed by revocation, covering both delivery and access changes.

Include originals, renditions, repeated encoding, compute, delivery requests, transfer, metadata, and telemetry. Sustaining 3.75 GB/s for one hour transfers 3.75 × 3,600 = 13,500 GB, or 13.5 TB, before overhead. ¶ Viewer-demand duration matters alongside peak concurrency. Compare profile count and retention with quality needs, using labelled fictional rates or verified quotes. A smaller rendition set saves work while changing the client's playback choices.

A single file is simple to manage and offers little adaptation. Segmented adaptive delivery adds manifests and a larger artifact lifecycle. Pre-encoding makes outputs ready early while spending resources on profiles some viewers never use. ¶ On-demand encoding reduces unused work and adds startup or burst risk. Managed conversion delegates selected operations, while publication, access, and reconciliation remain design decisions. Compare alternatives under equal client, privacy, and recovery requirements.

Start with resumable upload and durable processing identity. Add a small tested adaptive set with versioned publication. Introduce suitable edge delivery and grow encoding independently from playback. ¶ Add regional objects or profiles for demonstrated locality, recovery, or quality needs. Retain exact source and profile identities so each result remains explainable and reproducible. Increased capacity must preserve what playable, private, and deleted mean for existing videos.

Review three claims separately: every ready generation is playable, private artifacts remain protected, and origin survives edge failure. Each needs its own evidence. ¶ Test corrupt input, interruption between output and publication, repeated completion, missing segments, expired access, and deletion after caching. One successful warm-cache playback leaves these partial-processing and private-recovery paths unexamined.

Build a publisher that requires three segments. Write two and stop. The manifest must stay unpublished. Resume with the same job identity, write the third, validate checksums, and atomically advance one metadata generation. ¶ Next remove a published segment deliberately and run an integrity check. It should identify the broken set. Explain the difference between the boundary that established readiness and the later failure that damaged an already published generation.''',
['Resumable source upload',
 'Versioned isolated encoding jobs',
 'Validate and publish generation',
 'Authorized adaptive edge delivery'],
['Video owns source version',
 'Job references profile and epoch',
 'Rendition has immutable artifacts',
 'Manifest generation references complete set'],
'def publish(job):\n    require_current_epoch(job)\n    artifacts = expected_artifacts(job.source, job.profile)\n    require(all(exists_and_valid(a) for a in artifacts))\n    with transaction():\n        assert_current_epoch(job)\n        generation = record_complete_manifest(artifacts)\n        mark_playable(job.video, generation)\n# Partial worker output is never silently declared ready.')

case(7, '''A driver reports location and availability. A rider requests a trip; the service finds candidates, offers work, records an accepted assignment, and tracks progress or cancellation. ¶ A nearby driver is a candidate. Receiving an offer also does not establish assignment. Define offer expiry and what the rider sees while matching is unresolved. Two matchers observing the same old availability must not create incompatible active trips for one driver.

Set a bounded machine-side dispatch target for the assumed region and preserve exclusive active assignments. Treat position age and accuracy as uncertainty rather than ground truth, and protect precise location information. ¶ Trip transitions must survive retries and temporary disconnection. A missing heartbeat does not prove a trip has ended. Human offer acceptance and payment-provider outcomes follow different timelines. Keep those states visible so every delay does not become an unsupported failed-trip result.

Assume 100,000 online drivers report every five seconds. This produces 100,000 / 5 = 20,000 updates/s. At 100 raw bytes each, the stream is 2 MB/s, or 172.8 GB/day if every update is retained. ¶ Assume 100 new trip requests/s. Estimate map requests, candidate evaluation, regional skew, and retention separately. The high-volume location projection and lower-volume assignment authority have different jobs and need not use the same storage or coordination mechanism.

Begin with a service holding recent positions and selecting a nearby available driver. Sequential requests are easy to follow. With concurrent requests, two matchers may select the same driver before either changes availability. ¶ Keep search positions as a derived projection and perform the exclusive claim against authoritative assignment state. Improve spatial lookup after this boundary is correct. Faster nearest-neighbour search can produce better candidates, but cannot decide between simultaneous claims. Stale locations can still harm match quality without violating exclusive assignment.

Location updates include position, source sequence, and observed time. POST /trips creates a stable rider request. POST /offers/{id}/accept attempts a valid claim; status and cancellation expose explicit trip transitions. ¶ Derive driver identity from authentication and validate plausible updates. Expired or superseded offers return a documented conflict. Repeated acceptance recovers the existing result. A caller-supplied driver identifier must not bypass ownership checks or replace another assignment.

driver_state holds availability, assignment_id, and version. A trip holds rider, state, driver, and version. An offer identifies trip, driver, expiry, and status. Latest-position records include driver sequence, spatial cell, and observation time; retained location history has a separate restricted lifecycle. ¶ Protect active assignment through conditional transitions or suitable uniqueness constraints. A spatial index helps find candidates; it must not independently decide whether a driver is free.

LocationIngestor validates update order. SpatialIndex finds candidates, Matcher ranks a bounded set, and OfferService checks offer validity. AssignmentRepository owns the claim; TripStateMachine controls later transitions. ¶ Map and ETA adapters have separate deadlines so provider failure cannot corrupt assignment. Stable trip and acceptance identities make retries recover the same work. Many matchers may choose candidates, while the final availability decision stays at its atomic owner.

Use a regional location stream and spatial projection, durable trip and assignment storage, matching workers, offer delivery, and trip events. Geographic partitioning can keep searches local, provided driver movement has a defined handoff. ¶ Configuration may arrive from a control plane, but two regions with stale availability copies must not both assign one driver. Assignment requires one valid authority or a transfer protocol. Draw local search and exclusive ownership as distinct paths.

Commit the rider request identity. Search sufficiently fresh candidates, recheck authoritative availability, and create a bounded-lifetime offer. On acceptance, verify that the offer is still pending, unexpired, and compatible with current ownership. ¶ Atomically bind driver and trip under the chosen transaction model. Repeated acceptance recovers its result; stale acceptance conflicts. If the reply disappears, trip status resolves uncertainty. The conditional acceptance, rather than delivery of the offer, prevents incompatible winners.

The workload produces 20,000 position updates/s and 100 dispatches/s. Evaluating twenty candidates per dispatch adds 100 × 20 = 2,000 candidate evaluations/s before retries and ETA requests. ¶ Do not automatically call a routing service for every position update. Estimate hot cells, city-centre peaks, and boundary crossings. Candidate pruning can lower map cost while leaving the exclusive claim unchanged. Global averages can hide the one area where most work is concentrated.

Suppose ingress takes 30 ms, candidate lookup 40 ms, bounded ETA evaluation 80 ms, and assignment work 30 ms. The machine-side planning budget totals 180 ms. ¶ Human acceptance is a separate interval. Missing candidates, communication delays, expiry, and retries extend the whole matching journey. Measure time in those states and validate the budget against representative cities, traffic, and providers. An optimistic machine sum does not establish the rider's total wait.

An older position must not move the serving projection backward under its source-sequence rule. If two requests choose one driver, only one authoritative claim may win. A paused worker must not accept an offer superseded while it was stopped. ¶ Recover lost offers or replies through status. A map outage may allow a justified simpler ranking or require paused matching, but cannot bypass exclusive ownership. Exercise each failure at its state boundary so a search problem cannot become conflicting trip commitments.

Scale location ingestion and spatial serving separately from assignment transactions. Prune or batch ETA calls. When splitting regions or cells, transfer the driver's authority explicitly. ¶ Candidate caches need age information. Extra matchers cannot automatically divide a hot claim owner whose decisions remain serialised. Finer cells add boundary queries and movement coordination, so choose them from measured concentrated work while retaining one active-assignment rule.

Location can propagate through a delayed projection with a declared age policy. Assignment requires a coordinated conditional change linking driver and trip. Crossing a regional boundary requires authority transfer, rather than independent acceptance on each side. ¶ Give status reads the required read-after-accept behaviour. Observation time helps judge position age; sequence rules reject old updates, and fencing rules reject old owners. These mechanisms protect different kinds of state and should be explained separately.

Authenticate driver updates and limit rider and driver views to the relevant trip. Restrict precise history, including support and administrator paths, retain only justified data, and audit privileged use. ¶ Bound geographic scraping and costly map requests. Choosing an arbitrary driver identifier does not confer claim authority. Keep raw coordinates out of broad telemetry where unnecessary. Apply privacy rules to streams, caches, exports, and logs as well as the displayed map.

Use regional placement with a stated failure model and durable assignment state. Keep releases compatible with older clients and in-progress offers. Drain new work while retaining existing trips and offers. ¶ Recovery must reconcile active assignments and uncertain provider outcomes. An old snapshot must not reoffer a driver still assigned elsewhere. Establish valid history and fencing before replacement authority acts. Test recovery with active trips, since an empty-system restart cannot reveal conflicting ownership.

Track location age, stale-update rejection, cell skew, candidate counts, claim conflicts, offer expiry, matching outcomes, and time in each dispatch state. Observe map-provider errors separately. ¶ Correlate stable trip-creation and acceptance identities. Alert on unresolved assignments and invariant violations as well as HTTP failures. Simulated drivers can cross cells, disconnect, and accept near expiry while riders compete, testing fresh search and exclusive outcomes together.

Include ingestion, recent-location state, map and ETA calls, assignments, offer attempts, history, transfer, and operations. Route calls for every update could dominate cost despite efficient position storage. ¶ Model calls per candidate and dispatch, then compare pruning and batching using explicit fictional rates or verified terms. Keep the accuracy and latency effects of cheaper ranking visible. The model makes no claim about a real company's internal architecture or bill.

Straight-line distance is inexpensive and may ignore road travel. Route-based ETA can improve ranking while adding delay and a provider dependency. Sequential offers reduce simultaneous acceptance opportunities; multiple offers require a controlled single winner. ¶ Fine spatial cells can improve locality and add handoffs and boundary queries. Compare fairness, rider wait, candidate quality, and machine cost. Each choice must preserve exclusive assignment.

Start with a correct regional claim and basic proximity search. Add streamed positions and spatial indexes when lookup work requires them, then distribute matching with bounded ETA evaluation. ¶ Split geographic ownership after measuring hot areas and defining a safe transfer procedure. Regional expansion adds a failure and partition policy for active trips. Preserve request identities and claims throughout: replicating matcher processes does not replicate the authority on which they depend.

Review what nearby, recently observed, available, offered, and assigned each mean. A spatial index answers proximity and age questions, but cannot alone establish current ownership. ¶ Test competing riders, out-of-order positions, acceptance after expiry, paused workers, and handoff. One claim should win, and losers should receive meaningful state. Confirm that search projections never become independent writers of authoritative availability. A fast matching demonstration can otherwise conceal the decisive race.

Build an optimistic driver claim with an expected version and offer expiry. Start two requests from the same version. Verify that one local claim wins and the other conflicts. ¶ Deliver an older position and a delayed acceptance after the winner commits. Neither may overwrite the active assignment. Explain the boundary demonstrated: a local transaction establishes one local owner, while external trip or payment effects still require their own contracts and recovery evidence.''',
['Ordered driver position ingestion',
 'Fresh candidate search',
 'Conditional exclusive driver claim',
 'Durable trip state and recovery'],
['Driver has one active assignment',
 'Trip owns lifecycle state',
 'Offer has expiry and version',
 'Location is a versioned projection'],
'def accept_offer(offer, driver, now):\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_offer(offer.id)\n        require(current.pending and now < current.expires_at)\n        won = claim_driver_if_available(driver, current.expected_version)\n        if not won: raise AssignmentConflict\n        bind_trip_and_offer(offer.trip, driver, offer.id)\n    return current_trip_state(offer.trip)')

case(8, '''A buyer browses an event, holds selected seats, pays, and receives tickets. Holds last a defined period; cancellation and refunds follow explicit policies. ¶ The displayed seat map is advisory. The authoritative reservation decides who owns the opportunity to buy. Retries, expiry tasks, and payment callbacks can overlap, but must never confirm one seat for two buyers. Establish that invariant before improving the map or adding a waiting room.

Prevent duplicate confirmed ownership and retain accepted payment and ticket records under the selected failure contract. Pending holds must resolve through supported confirmation, expiry, or recovery. ¶ Set launch admission and waiting objectives, including fairness. Fixed inventory cannot satisfy every simultaneous request. Queue or reject excess demand clearly. Distinguish an admitted transaction's delay from the buyer's total time waiting for admission and payment.

Assume 50,000 seats and a short peak of 100,000 reservation attempts/s. At 200 raw bytes each, seat records occupy about 10 MB before indexes. ¶ The small inventory size hides heavy contention: many callers want the same ownership changes. Assume two-minute holds and a bounded seat count per user. Estimate retries, conflicts, and expiry work separately. This launch scenario is a teaching workload rather than a database benchmark.

Start with a transactional seat table. Change available to reserved conditionally and insert the reservation in the same transaction. Check whether the claim actually affected the seat. ¶ With a naive read and unconditional write, two buyers can both read available and both report success. The conditional transition makes one winner visible to the other. Once this boundary is correct, address flash demand, payment uncertainty, and expiry races. A faster interface alone would only accelerate the incorrect history.

POST /reservations includes event, seat identities, and idempotency key. The returned resource gives status and expiry. A payment operation belongs to that reservation; authorised ticket reads expose committed issuance. ¶ Bind repeat keys to trusted actor and content so a retry returns the earlier result. Distinguish provider authorisation, capture, decline, and uncertainty under its contract. An expired hold and an uncertain payment require different next actions and should remain distinct states.

A seat stores event and seat identity, state, reservation owner, and version. A reservation stores owner, state, expiry, version, and its seat relationships. Payments and tickets have separate identities. ¶ Enforce one relevant confirmed active ticket per seat. Expiry and confirmation check current reservation identity and state/version. A delayed expiry task for an old hold must not release a seat acquired by a newer reservation. Use actual constraints or atomic checks to preserve these relationships.

SeatRepository owns conditional claims or locks. ReservationService applies selection and hold rules. PaymentWorkflow coordinates external effects, TicketIssuer commits valid issuance, and ExpiryWorker conditionally releases old holds. ¶ Represent prices and refunds with explicit amounts and units. Put ownership checks at the shared authoritative boundary, where callbacks and workers also pass them. Return enough state to distinguish conflict, expiry, confirmed success, and provider uncertainty.

Put bounded admission or a waiting room before the ownership store. Keep durable reservations, a payment adapter, workflow or outbox progress, and ticket reads. Cache event descriptions and advisory seat maps separately. ¶ Partitioning by event can keep its transactions local, but a popular event can remain hot. Admission and contention controls still matter. The cache suggests what may be available; the transaction establishes what this buyer reserved. They must not become competing allocation authorities.

Atomically claim seats and create the hold, then return its expiry after commit. Submit payment with a stable provider identity. Confirmation verifies a still-valid transition and commits the required reservation, seat, and ticket changes. ¶ If payment succeeds after expiry, apply the defined recovery: safely reacquire if current ownership permits, or refund or follow another supported resolution. Never issue against another buyer's seat. Expiry checks current identity and version before release so delayed work cannot undo a later valid state.

Suppose launch admission permits 2,000 attempts/s from an offered 100,000/s. The rest wait or are rejected under policy. The 2,000 limit is a configured input to validate, not demonstrated capacity. ¶ Seat inventory bounds concurrent holds, but does not bound attempts or retries during a two-minute hold. Estimate writes per attempt, duplicate-key checks, conflicts, and expiry scans. Web concurrency may grow rapidly while the same small seat set remains contested.

Suppose the admitted path takes 20 ms at the edge, 30 ms acquiring transaction resources, 40 ms for conditional seat and reservation work, and 20 ms returning the reply. The budget totals 110 ms. ¶ Contention may exceed it, so bound waiting and stop work that is no longer useful. Measure waiting-room time separately. Payment has another expiry-sensitive timeline. Quick admitted transactions do not establish a quick booking journey for buyers still queued outside allocation.

Only one buyer may win after both read available. If reservation commit succeeds but its reply disappears, the repeat key recovers the hold. Repeated payment evidence must not issue another ticket. ¶ Test late payment and stale expiry after confirmation or reassignment. Conditional transitions and reconciliation handle their overlapping histories. If ticket email fails after issuance commits, the ticket still exists and needs another delivery route. A communication failure must not undo accepted ownership.

Cache advisory data, bound admission, and keep the correct seat transaction short. Partition independent events when measured work justifies it. ¶ Multi-seat claims need an explicit all-or-nothing rule and consistent lock order. Splitting one event across shards complicates group booking, and extra shards do not divide contention for one seat. Scale surrounding work while preserving the atomic owner. Adopt a more complex allocation protocol only for a demonstrated benefit.

Seat ownership and ticket issuance need an atomic authoritative rule. Displays may lag if buyers understand their advisory status. Payment and email remain external effects with stable identities and tracked progress. ¶ A serializable local transaction can protect local concurrency; it does not include a provider automatically. Retry serialization failures through bounded whole-transaction retries with safe external-effect placement. State which records change together and which outcomes are reconciled later.

Authorise holds, payments, tickets, transfers, and refunds. Calculate prices server-side, verify provider callbacks, protect credentials, and limit automated abuse under a defined identity policy. ¶ Scope idempotency keys so one actor cannot recover another's result. Give ticket capabilities appropriate scope and lifetime, and audit important changes. A waiting room controls capacity; identity, permissions, and fairness require their own enforcement and tests.

Keep states and expiry meanings compatible during rollouts. Durable expiry survives worker replacement, and database promotion must respect the acknowledged-data rule. ¶ After recovery, reconcile provider evidence before declaring bookings resolved. If write authority is uncertain, pausing allocation may preserve exclusivity while descriptions remain readable. Test with live holds and callbacks. An old restored snapshot cannot establish permission to sell seats confirmed after that snapshot.

Track admitted attempts, claim conflicts, transaction waits, hold age, expiry backlog, payment uncertainty, late successes, and ticket uniqueness. Follow each logical booking through retries and callbacks. ¶ Check invariants across seats, holds, and tickets and compare them with payment evidence. Empty error logs cannot prove that the relationships agree. Measure user-visible waiting and completion separately from raw API latency.

Include burst admission, transactions, abandoned holds, payment calls, ticket delivery, retention, telemetry, and recovery capacity. Use the launch's duration rather than applying its peak for the entire month. ¶ More web workers cannot remove contention on one seat. Compare designs under equal no-double-ticket and recovery requirements using explicit fictional rates or verified prices. Include retry and expiry work even for buyers who abandon purchase.

Pessimistic locking serialises early and may cause waiting or deadlocks. Optimistic conditional claims produce conflicts that callers must handle clearly. One event owner simplifies order while imposing a capacity boundary. ¶ Distributed allocation can reduce some central work and complicate group-seat atomicity or fairness. First explain why one buyer wins under the chosen mechanism, then integrate that winner with payment and expiry. Acquiring a lock alone does not finish the booking workflow.

First implement the seat transaction and repeat-key result. Add durable expiry and payment reconciliation, then launch admission and advisory caching. ¶ Partition independent events after measurement. Change one-event allocation only when demonstrated limits justify the added protocol. Recheck no-double-ticket behaviour at every stage, including old expiry and retry paths. Additional throughput must preserve the earlier safe ownership decisions.

Check that expiry and confirmation refer to the same current reservation identity and state. Review successful payment after the hold is gone, and verify that displayed availability never grants ownership. ¶ Test competing buyers, repeated callbacks, commit-then-crash, stale expiry after reassignment, and late payment. A correct happy-path seat update covers only one boundary. The review must explain money and tickets still unresolved after a request ends.

Reproduce the last-seat race using two connections or an explicit interleaving. Let both buyers read available, then demonstrate the incorrect unconditional writes. Replace them with a conditional claim and reservation transaction, checking affected rows. ¶ Add versioned expiry. Confirm or reassign the seat, then deliver the old expiry task. It must leave the current owner's seat unchanged. Identify the checked identity and version that make this safe, independently of usual task timing.''',
['Admit bounded booking attempt',
 'Atomic seat reservation and expiry',
 'Stable payment operation',
 'Conditional ticket issuance or compensation'],
['Event owns seats',
 'Reservation owns held seat set',
 'Payment operation has stable identity',
 'One ticket per event-seat invariant'],
'with transaction():\n    insert_reservation(reservation_id, owner, expires_at)\n    updated = reserve_if_available(event, seat, reservation_id)\n    if updated != 1: raise SeatUnavailable\n# Expiry later releases only the same current reservation version.\n# Provider payment is coordinated outside this local transaction.')

case(9, '''A client creates a payment intent. The service requests authorisation or capture, reports supported outcomes, records balanced accounting entries, and reconciles provider evidence. Refunds have separate operation identities and progress. ¶ Represent money with currency, integer minor units, and an explicit rounding policy. An order's status, a provider operation, and the ledger are related records, but cannot be replaced by one paid flag. The design must explain their sequence, uncertainty, and accounting consequences.

Preserve financial invariants and auditable state. Prevent duplicate relevant provider effects where its contract permits that guarantee, and resolve uncertainty from evidence rather than inventing success or decline. ¶ Measure intent acceptance separately from completion. Under the chosen accounting model, each settled amount needs unique balanced postings and authorised corrections. During a provider outage, a durable pending state can be the correct result. Fast unsupported finality would leave an outcome the system cannot explain later.

Assume one million intents/day, about 11.6/s, and an illustrative 200/s peak. Suppose each initially produces six operational or audit records of 1 kB. That is six million kB, or 6 GB/day raw. ¶ Ledger details, callbacks, indexes, copies, and retention add storage. Provider concurrency and reconciliation traffic need separate estimates, especially during long waits or recovery. These synthetic assumptions make no claim about actual provider fees or institutional architecture.

A simple service calls a provider, sets an order flag, and replies. If charging succeeds and the process dies before saving the result, a retry may charge again. ¶ Add a durable operation identity and explicit uncertainty before scaling. Local transactions protect related local changes. Provider keys, status queries, and reconciliation address the gap outside that transaction. Each protects a different part of the history. More instances cannot recover an ambiguous effect for which no stable identity was kept.

POST /payment-intents accepts amount_minor, currency, business reference, and idempotency key. Status exposes supported states such as pending, authorised, captured, declined, or uncertain. A refund endpoint creates a separately keyed refund. ¶ Bind keys to actor and financial payload; changed content under an existing key conflicts. Verify provider webhooks instead of trusting client approval fields. Define how clients wait or query uncertainty so a missing reply does not automatically start another financial operation.

payment_intent and operation records retain actor, key, fingerprint, progress, and result. Provider attempts keep stable keys, references, and outcomes. A verified-event inbox deduplicates evidence. ¶ Ledger transactions own entries with account, currency, and signed amount. Within each required currency and accounting scope, the signed entries sum to zero: the amount debited is matched by the amount credited. Enforce operation linkage and correction identity. An authorised correction adds a new accounting event rather than rewriting past evidence, keeping the financial history reviewable.

PaymentService controls transitions; Money and policy values validate amounts. ProviderAdapter maps explicit outcomes, OperationRepository recognises duplicates, and LedgerService validates and commits postings. Reconciler compares provider evidence; RefundWorkflow tracks refunds. ¶ These roles can begin inside one application. Map a timeout to uncertainty, not definite decline. Hide protocol details at the adapter while retaining evidence needed for recovery. A useful boundary clarifies meaning and ownership without requiring a network service for every responsibility.

Use an authenticated API, durable intent and operation storage, bounded provider workers, verified callbacks, a ledger boundary, and scheduled reconciliation. Commit any required downstream publication intent with the relevant local change. ¶ Reserve reconciliation capacity so it can repair uncertainty during incidents. Regional serving must preserve provider-operation and ledger authority. Draw provider calls outside the local transaction boundary: this makes the external gap and its recovery path visible.

Commit intent and logical operation identity, then submit its stable provider key. Record supported evidence or uncertainty. Capture or settlement evidence drives an idempotent transition that creates the required balanced postings once. ¶ Callbacks can precede the synchronous reply, so valid transitions must handle either order without regressing. Reconciliation queries the stable reference and repairs discrepancies. A local pending row is not permission to resend blindly. Trace each evidence and commit boundary to identify what a crash can leave unresolved.

At a stable 200 provider calls/s and an assumed average 500 ms wait, Little's Law gives 200 × 0.5 = 100 in-flight calls within that boundary. ¶ This estimates average occupancy, not a safe pool size. Account for variance, deadlines, provider limits, and failures. Initial records add 6 GB/day raw. Callbacks and reconciliation continue after request traffic subsides, so they need their own capacity instead of only the daily-average allowance.

Suppose ingress takes 20 ms, intent commit 20 ms, provider work 500 ms, and final local update 20 ms. The successful synchronous budget totals 560 ms. ¶ Returning durable pending after about 40 ms is a different contract with later completion. Neither sum establishes p99. A timeout calls for status recovery or reconciliation; it does not establish that no payment occurred. Measure acceptance and final resolution separately.

Test lost replies after local commit, provider execution followed by process loss, reordered or repeated callbacks, uncertain refunds, and posting-commit failure. ¶ Keep unknown outcomes and repeat only through a supported safe identity or established evidence. If the ledger is unavailable, setting paid bypasses a required boundary rather than repairing it. Recovery must account for provider state and required postings before declaring the operation resolved. A restarted healthy API does not establish agreement between those histories.

Scale independent acceptance and workers while bounding provider concurrency and preserving per-payment and relevant per-account invariants. Isolate enough reconciliation capacity to repair uncertain operations during trouble. ¶ Read projections can expose status under a stated freshness rule. Ledger partitioning requires an explicit balancing and transaction scope; a hot account may remain concentrated. Partition only for a demonstrated bottleneck, since additional ownership boundaries also add recovery work.

Local deduplication, payment transitions, and required postings need suitable atomic boundaries. The provider remains outside them and requires its own identity and recovery contract. ¶ Uniqueness and transactions can establish one local posting set without proving one provider execution. Refunds have independent state and may not reverse timing, fees, or downstream consequences perfectly. Define the evidence for each accepted result and retain uncertainty until a valid final transition is supported.

Authorise payments and refunds, validate amounts server-side, verify callbacks, and protect credentials. Avoid retaining unnecessary sensitive data through an appropriately reviewed provider integration or tokenisation arrangement. ¶ Restrict and audit privileged corrections. Operators need explicit authority and must not silently rewrite accounting history. Scope effects and queries to tenant and account. Preserve useful identity and outcomes in logs without secrets. These controls do not by themselves assert any financial or legal compliance status.

Evolve states and event formats compatibly, retain operation identities during releases and recovery, and control provider configuration and key changes. ¶ An old restore may forget keys for effects already executed externally. Establish replay protection and reconcile before resubmission. Test delayed callbacks from earlier releases and recovery after outages. Restored pending rows must be compared with provider evidence before being treated as untouched work.

Separate acceptance and completion delay. Track age of pending and uncertain work, attempts, duplicate events, callback verification, imbalances, unmatched settlements, refunds, and reconciliation outcomes. ¶ Alert on invariant violations and old unresolved money movement even when HTTP success is high. Evidence must distinguish attempted, provider-accepted, and locally posted states. Stable identities let an investigator reconstruct those stages without treating one response or log line as proof of the entire journey.

Include provider attempts, secure storage, ledger work, retained audit evidence, telemetry, reconciliation, and operations. Use symbolic fees or labelled hypothetical inputs unless prices are verified. ¶ Compare cost per reconciled successful business payment as well as per HTTP attempt. Retries can increase attempts while useful outcomes decline. Designs must preserve the same financial invariants; an inexpensive system that cannot explain uncertain charges fails a required part of the service.

Synchronous completion may suit clients expecting immediate results and exposes provider waits. Asynchronous intents make pending progress explicit while adding workflow state. ¶ A local transactional application simplifies related invariants. Separating provider and ledger services may improve ownership or isolation and adds coordination. Append-only accounting history can coexist with mutable status projections. Compare boundaries by the work and recovery they clarify instead of forcing one paid record to serve every purpose.

Start with explicit states, currency-aware integer amounts, local transactions, and stable provider identity. Add durable callbacks and reconciliation before multiplying traffic paths. ¶ Then introduce projections and bounded workers for measured needs. Partition only where a demonstrated limit justifies a preserved invariant boundary. Earlier payments must remain explainable through evidence and postings after topology changes. Increased capacity must retain that historical meaning.

Ask what evidence establishes financial success, who commits the postings, and how recovery handles an older deduplication snapshot beside newer external effects. ¶ Test charge-then-crash, callback-before-reply, duplicate evidence, partial refunds, and earlier-state restore. Check for one valid local posting set and explicit unresolved state where evidence is missing. A timeout cannot be turned into decline merely to make the request path appear complete.

Build a synthetic provider that remembers stable keys and a local ledger accepting only balanced entries. Stop after provider execution and before recording the local result. ¶ Reconcile by key and verify that one logical operation produces one balanced posting set. Replay its event and reject changed financial content under the old key. Explain that the test demonstrates this synthetic provider contract and local boundary, rather than a guarantee about arbitrary real providers.''',
['Durable payment intent and key',
 'Provider effect may be uncertain',
 'Verified evidence reconciles state',
 'Atomic balanced ledger postings'],
['Payment owns operation identity',
 'Provider attempt has external reference',
 'Ledger transaction owns balanced entries',
 'Refund has independent lifecycle'],
'def apply_settlement(event):\n    with transaction():\n        if inbox_contains(event.id): return prior_outcome(event.id)\n        payment = lock_payment(event.payment_id)\n        validate_transition_and_amount(payment, event)\n        entries = [debit_entry(event), credit_entry(event)]\n        require_balanced_by_currency(entries)\n        insert_unique_posting_set(event.operation_id, entries)\n        update_payment_and_insert_inbox(payment, event)\n# Provider execution is reconciled separately from this transaction.')

case(10, '''A user creates a schedule, the service identifies due occurrences, and workers execute jobs. Users inspect history, configure retries, and cancel supported future work. ¶ Define fixed times versus intervals, timezone handling, missed runs, and overlap. One schedule can produce many occurrences; retrying an occurrence must not invent another logical run for the same due time. Establish this timing meaning before choosing a timer or queue.

Keep accepted schedules and required runs recoverable under supported failures. Bound scheduling delay and prevent superseded attempts from overwriting newer results. Execution may start repeatedly around failure, so effects need suitable identity or enforced fencing. ¶ Define cancellation's boundary. Stopping future dispatch, cancelling pending work, and attempting to interrupt a running effect are different promises. A local cancellation flag does not establish that a remote effect was undone. Run status must preserve this distinction.

Assume one million schedules produce 100,000 occurrences/hour. Dividing by 3,600 gives about 27.8 runs/s. At an initial 2 kB of state per run, raw growth is 200 MB/hour. ¶ With ten-second average execution at a stable rate, average active work is 27.8 × 10, or about 278 runs. Top-of-hour bursts, long jobs, retries, and retention add requirements. Saved-schedule count alone does not describe the concentration of due work.

One process scans due schedules and launches local functions. If it saves a run and advances the schedule in separate commits, a crash between them can repeat or miss an occurrence. ¶ Create a stable occurrence record and advance due state in one transaction. A leader can simplify scanning, but leadership alone does not make worker effects occur once. First establish durable identity and atomic progress. Adding another process to the unsafe loop merely creates more opportunities for the same failure.

POST /schedules accepts timing, timezone, payload reference, retry rules, and overlap policy. Cancellation changes documented future behaviour. GET /runs/{id} reports attempts and outcome. A run-once operation has its own idempotency identity. ¶ Bound timing-expression and payload complexity. Return durable acceptance separately from execution completion. If an external effect is unresolved, report uncertainty. A worker's missing reply does not prove that its effect never happened.

A schedule stores id, version, next_due, timing policy, and state. A run binds unique (schedule_id,occurrence) to input version and progress. Attempts contain epoch, lease deadline, worker, and result. Retain original due time separately from dispatch time. ¶ An epoch numbers successive owners so completion can reject an old attempt. Another attempt still belongs to the same logical run. Outbox and effect records retain durable dispatch and external identities where needed.

ScheduleParser and TimePolicy calculate occurrences. DueScanner locates candidates. RunRepository saves the run and next_due atomically. Dispatcher routes work, LeaseManager grants attempts, and WorkerAdapter executes them. Reconciler finds stuck or uncertain outcomes. ¶ The scheduler decides when work is due; a worker acts under a current bounded claim. Each effect adapter needs explicit repetition or fencing semantics. This separation lets tests examine time calculations without accidentally performing an irreversible external operation.

Keep durable schedules, coordinated scanners or partition owners, dispatch records, bounded workers, attempt state, and reconciliation. Store large versioned payloads separately rather than copying them into each retry message. ¶ Timing control and execution have different capacity and failure paths. A control-plane outage may delay new dispatch while existing jobs continue. Worker loss must leave a recoverable run. A queue attached to a cron loop does not supply these behaviours without durable state and ownership rules.

A scanner claims due state, creates the unique occurrence and dispatch intent, advances next_due, and commits. A worker then claims an attempt epoch and executes under the stable run identity. It renews its lease where supported and completes only while current. ¶ After expiry, a replacement may take over. The old worker may still resume, so effect-side identity or fencing must limit harmful stale actions. Reconcile runs without an acceptable terminal state. Starting a callback is an intermediate event, rather than the end of the durable history.

At about 27.8 runs/s with ten-second average execution, average concurrency is roughly 278. If each worker sustains four independent I/O jobs, 278 / 4 is about 69.5: roughly seventy workers before headroom and other limits. ¶ CPU-heavy jobs require measured compute capacity instead. Estimate burst concentration, retries, and outage catch-up. A steady-state average cannot establish that the same worker count meets start-time objectives during a large due-time burst.

Scheduling delay runs from due time to dispatch or start. Runtime begins after execution starts. As an illustrative target, require 95% of eligible runs to start within five seconds under supported load. ¶ Scan intervals, claims, queues, cold starts, and concurrency limits consume that budget. A ten-minute task can meet a five-second start target. Give completion deadlines a separate rule and measure both when required, rather than combining all durations into an ambiguous job-latency figure.

Test interruption between occurrence creation and schedule advancement; the transaction must leave both committed or neither. Then pause a worker past its lease so it overlaps a replacement. The current-epoch check must reject the resumed worker's local completion. ¶ This does not undo external effects already accepted. Those require effect-side protection and reconciliation. Also define clock changes, timezone transitions, malformed input, and poison-job handling. Correct due-time arithmetic alone does not establish safe repeated execution.

Index due time, claim bounded batches, and partition schedules with stable ownership when scanning is limiting. Scale workers according to measured CPU, memory, and I/O demands. ¶ Separate long, priority, and CPU-heavy work where necessary. Bound catch-up so overdue runs do not starve current critical work. Removing a global lock must preserve atomic occurrence creation through another valid mechanism. Lower contention is useful only if it retains the logical-run invariant.

Creating an occurrence and advancing the schedule require uniqueness and atomicity. Worker delivery can repeat. Current-epoch completion protects local progress, while effect identity or resource-enforced fencing protects the relevant external effect. ¶ A lease is permission valid for an interval; it does not stop a paused process from resuming. It therefore cannot alone guarantee one execution. With fencing, the protected resource recognises current authority and rejects requests carrying stale epochs; the receiver must actually enforce that rule. Define missed-run and overlap policies so incidental timing does not decide which logical runs exist. Schedule, occurrence, attempt, and effect are distinct scopes.

Authorise schedule ownership, payload references, run views, and cancellation. Support validated work types rather than arbitrary code or unrestricted URLs supplied through the API. ¶ Bind each run to tenant and immutable input. Give workers only required capabilities and audit administration. A broad administrator credential in every worker makes one compromised job unnecessarily powerful. Follow permission from schedule through task to the actual resource used by execution.

Keep schedule and run formats compatible during rollout, including epoch and lease meanings. Drain new claims and save progress before shutdown. During recovery, compare queues with authoritative runs. ¶ An older restored schedule table may lag effects already executed externally. Recover stable occurrence identity and evidence before replaying. Test control-plane loss separately from worker loss. Restarting scans must restore the meaning of due work and prior outcomes, rather than merely resume timer activity.

Measure due-to-dispatch and due-to-start delay, oldest eligible work, repeated attempts, lease expiry, stale-completion rejection, retries, duration, and tenant fairness. Verify that one occurrence maps to one logical run. ¶ CPU may remain low when a broken scanner creates no work. Monitor generation and progress directly, alerting on accepted jobs that stop advancing. Detailed histories must distinguish runs from attempts so operational repetition is not confused with new scheduled demand.

Include scanning, indexes, history, dispatch, execution, spare capacity, external calls, and recovery. At 2 kB/run and 100,000 runs/hour, raw daily history is 200 MB/hour × 24 = 4.8 GB before attempts and indexes. ¶ Compare scan frequency and batching against the start-delay requirement. Use explicit rates and include catch-up and idle headroom. A lower cost obtained by silently missing occurrences does not satisfy the same service contract.

Polling an indexed due-time field is simple and recoverable. Timer wheels and delayed-message facilities may improve some timing workloads, provided they can be durably reconstructed. Central scanning simplifies authority; partitioned scanning adds coordination while dividing work. ¶ Long-lived workers reduce startup cost. Managed invocation may fit bursts under different limits. Compare timing precision, duration, recovery, and effects. A fast timer data structure solves due lookup, leaving occurrence and attempt semantics to the rest of the design.

Begin with deterministic time policy and atomic occurrence creation. Add durable dispatch and bounded execution. Introduce leases and epochs together with safe effect identity before claiming supported worker takeover. ¶ Partition whichever scan or execution path measurements identify. Regional ownership needs transfer and partition rules for due work. Preserve identities throughout, so replacements do not reinterpret retries as new occurrences or lose the evidence needed to resolve older runs.

Review occurrence identity, next_due atomicity, stale workers, cancellation, and recovery from older state. Each can leave a different gap. ¶ Run competing scanners at commit boundaries, pause a worker past expiry, cancel during execution, cross a timezone transition, and recover after a long outage. Check which runs should exist and which effects remain uncertain. One on-time callback demonstrates timing for one run, rather than the scheduler's full recovery contract.

Use an injected clock and transactional run table. Start two scanners for the same due occurrence and verify that they establish one logical run. ¶ Pause an epoch-7 attempt, replace it with epoch 8, and submit the old completion. The local store must reject it. Identify the stable key or receiver-enforced fencing still needed for an external effect. The exercise demonstrates local progress protection without claiming that the job physically executes only once.''',
['Atomic due occurrence creation',
 'Durable dispatch of stable run ID',
 'Worker claims attempt epoch',
 'Conditional completion and reconciliation'],
['Schedule has many occurrences',
 'Occurrence identity is UNIQUE',
 'Run has many attempts',
 'Attempt epoch fences local completion'],
"with transaction():\n    schedule = claim_due_schedule(now)\n    run = insert_unique_run(schedule.id, schedule.next_due)\n    insert_dispatch_outbox(run.id)\n    advance_next_due(schedule)\n# Worker completion compares the current attempt epoch atomically.\n# A stale worker's external effects need resource-side protection.")

case(11, '''Collaborators create a document, edit together, reconnect after disconnection, recover required history, and export or delete under access rules. ¶ First choose the document model. Plain text, structured rich text, and embedded objects have different valid operations and conflicts. Define those operations before selecting their transport. Cursor positions and presence are temporary hints, separate from durable content and its history.

Preserve acknowledged operations and enforce a defined access-revocation policy. Online editing should respond locally and meet a supported synchronization-delay objective. ¶ The protocol must converge under its assumptions, but equal final strings alone do not establish intended edits, Unicode correctness, or structural validity. A returning offline user may have lost access. Decide how unaccepted edits are rejected or presented, and distinguish them from accepted history. Optimistic local display is not a durable acknowledgment.

Assume 100,000 active documents, with 10,000 edited simultaneously by three users each. At two operations/s per user, ingestion is 10,000 × 3 × 2 = 60,000 operations/s. ¶ At 200 retained bytes each, this is 12 MB/s, or 1.0368 TB/day if sustained throughout the day. Actual activity duration, batching, snapshots, metadata, fan-out, and retention change the estimate. Peak concurrency must not silently become a full-day assumption in a real storage plan.

Start with whole-document updates carrying an expected version. If two writers replace the same version, one succeeds and the other conflicts. This is a clear simple contract, but awkward for continuous collaboration. ¶ An operation-based protocol handles smaller edits and their concurrency. Use a reviewed operational transformation (OT) or conflict-free replicated data type (CRDT) model suited to the document structure. Arbitrary patches applied by arrival order are insufficient: operation meaning, context, identity, and reconnect rules must work together.

Creation returns document identity. Sync messages carry document and replica identities, stable operation IDs, known progress, and protocol version. Snapshots return a versioned base and specify the history they already cover. ¶ Authorise exports and membership changes separately. Prevent clients from impersonating another replica. Define acknowledgments and reconnect so each edit is recognisably accepted, pending, or rejected. A successful connection handshake does not authorise every later edit or export.

Store owner, protocol version, membership, accepted operation IDs, ordering or causal context, and snapshots with covered positions. Compaction metadata records what can safely be retired. ¶ Sequence CRDTs need stable element IDs and ordering rules. OT needs transformation context for its supported offline interval. A snapshot must identify already included operations; otherwise, a receiver may omit or repeat work even when snapshot and log files are each intact.

DocumentService owns identity and membership. SyncCoordinator validates protocol use, EditModel applies its rules, and OperationLog persists acceptance. SnapshotBuilder produces recovery bases; Compactor retires only permitted history. Presence uses a temporary path. ¶ Keep the deterministic edit core apart from rendering and transport. Tests can then deliver the same operations in controlled orders. Expose durable progress explicitly, so a gateway reply is not mistaken for a committed edit.

Use authenticated gateways, client replicas, durable operation storage, snapshots, and the ordering or merge arrangement required by the selected algorithm. ¶ OT commonly uses ordered server context and transformation. CRDT approaches rely on their defined identities and merge rules. These families cannot be substituted as labels on an otherwise undefined box. Select a supported protocol per document version and plan transitions. Gateway replacement must preserve its required history and context.

A client applies an allowed edit locally and assigns a stable identity. The server checks access and protocol context, saves accepted work, then distributes and acknowledges it according to the contract. ¶ Reconnect exchanges missing operations or a compatible snapshot and continuation. Recognise repeats by identity and surface rejected offline changes explicitly. This sequence preserves local responsiveness while giving durable acceptance and access decisions their own boundaries. It also tells users which visible work still needs synchronization.

The assumed 60,000 operations/s at 200 bytes produces 12 MB/s raw history. Fan-out and extra copies multiply the work. One popular document may concentrate transformation, merge metadata, and gateway traffic despite many cold documents elsewhere. ¶ Snapshots reduce replay work in exchange for storage and background compute. Measure batching, document size, offline duration, and tombstone growth. Tombstones retain information about deleted elements where the protocol still needs their identities. Size the hot editor separately from the average.

Local optimistic editing can update the screen before a round trip while indicating unsynchronised work. Suppose ingress takes 30 ms, durable acceptance 20 ms, and peer delivery 50 ms. The proposed sync path totals 100 ms before additional waiting. ¶ Measure p99 separately. Distance, disconnection, and large documents alter the path. Track pending duration as well as frame responsiveness: a smooth local screen does not prove an edit survives loss of that client.

Concurrent inserts need a deterministic interpretation, and repeated delivery must not add a character twice. Missing context, unsafe compaction, and incompatible snapshots can break reconnect even after ordinary online editing works. ¶ Test stale membership and expensive malformed operations too. Keep enough privacy-safe failing-history context to reproduce interleavings. A brief convergence demonstration does not cover long partitions or retired metadata. Recovery assumptions must match the offline behaviour advertised to users.

Distribute independent documents, batch suitable operations, bound gateway buffers, and use snapshots to shorten replay. A single hot document may need its own coordination and fan-out design rather than another ordinary hash partition. ¶ Compaction must retain context for the supported offline and merge horizon. An apparently unused old identifier can still anchor a returning operation. Scale cursor updates separately from durable edits, since their retention and correctness requirements differ even over one connection.

OT transforms an operation against relevant concurrent operations. For example, an insertion before a later edit can shift that edit's position; the transform must use the correct ordered context. A sequence CRDT can instead name stable elements and define a shared order for concurrent insertions after the same element. ¶ If X and Y are inserted after one element, replicas need compatible identities and tie-breaking rules to produce the same sequence despite opposite arrival orders. Neither family automatically specifies rich text, undo, or user intent. Access and durable acknowledgment are additional properties. Select and test an actual algorithm with explicit assumptions.

Authorise sync sessions and individual operations under the revocation policy. Long-lived connections and returning offline clients require checks at later boundaries. Protect exports and snapshots too. ¶ Validate protocol inputs and avoid content leaks through presence or logs. End-to-end encryption changes server search, validation, retention, and revocation possibilities; design those consequences when required. TLS protects transport, but does not itself provide that document-level guarantee.

Negotiate protocol versions and keep accepted history interpretable. Never reuse an operation identity with silently changed meaning. Gateway replacement must rely on durable recovery, rather than one client's memory. ¶ Validate snapshots before retiring source history and test restoration from saved artifacts. Protocol migration and rollback affect stored documents and offline context as well as code. An older executable that misinterprets accepted operations can damage content that remains durably stored.

Measure pending and accepted edits, sync delay, duplicate recognition, reconnect gaps, snapshot age, replay time, compaction growth, protocol rejection, and access denial. ¶ Keep reproducible histories for failing interleavings and test convergence alongside permission and structural validity. User pending duration can reveal harm hidden by gateway latency. Use limited diagnostic artifacts and privacy-safe identities instead of copying private documents into routine traces.

Include history, snapshots, connections, fan-out, protocol compute, compaction, and telemetry. Replace sustained-rate arithmetic with measured activity durations when estimating expenditure. ¶ Longer offline support can require more context and metadata. Compare designs at an equal recovery horizon. Deleting information still needed by returning clients changes the contract. Storage savings therefore need a valid compaction rule, rather than an arbitrary age threshold.

Whole-document versions suit limited collaboration with explicit conflicts. OT supports particular transformation and ordering models. CRDTs provide specified merges with identity, metadata, and cleanup costs. ¶ A tested editing library may be preferable to a custom algorithm. Evaluate Unicode graphemes, selections, undo, rich-text structure, and offline context as well as final convergence. Choose a complete model the team can verify and maintain; no family supplies every editing behaviour automatically.

Begin with versioned documents and clear conflicts. Add a tested operation model, durable log, and reconnect. Introduce snapshots and justified compaction, then distribute independent documents. ¶ Isolate hot workloads and add regional or offline merging only under supported protocol assumptions. Preserve identities, access rules, and deterministic regression histories. Each stage must retain the meaning of accepted edits and the context older supported clients need for recovery.

Review convergence, supported offline duration, history retirement, and revocation. Deliver concurrent inserts in opposite orders, repeat a delete, lose an acknowledgment, and reconnect using an old snapshot plus later operations. ¶ Reconnect a revoked user and check document structure separately. Equal strings may conceal forbidden edits or invalid structure. Name the protocol and assumptions supporting each claim, rather than treating the final string comparison as complete validation.

Implement a miniature merge of two insertions with stable IDs and an explicit tie-break rule. Deliver both orders and repeat each operation. Verify equal final sequences with no duplicate insertion. ¶ State the exercise's limit: it is not a complete rich-text CRDT. Add snapshot-context and revoked-client checks to expose distinct recovery and access boundaries. The example demonstrates identity and deterministic ordering without claiming production-editor correctness from two operations.''',
['Authorized local edit with identity',
 'Protocol orders or merges operations',
 'Durable log acknowledges accepted work',
 'Snapshot and missing-operation recovery'],
['Document owns protocol version',
 'Operation has stable ID and context',
 'Snapshot records covered progress',
 'Membership applies to every sync'],
'def receive(operation, document):\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_document(document.id)\n        require_authorized(operation.actor, current)\n        require_protocol_version(operation, current)\n        if log.contains(operation.id): return prior_ack(operation.id)\n        next_state = edit_model.apply(current.state, operation)\n        append_unique_operation(operation)\n        commit_state_and_progress(current, next_state)\n    return durable_ack(operation.id)\n# The edit model must implement a specified, tested algorithm.')

case(12, '''A tenant uploads a document. The platform classifies it, extracts fields, sends uncertain results to review, and publishes validated output for authorised search or export. Users inspect progress, correct results, reprocess versions, and request deletion. ¶ Upload success establishes accepted bytes under the upload contract. Extraction, review, and publication remain separate work. Give each stage a visible state so users can tell which output is usable and what remains unresolved.

Preserve accepted inputs and required workflow progress under selected failures. Results identify exact source and pipeline versions. Protect tenant data and set processing-age objectives for supported inputs. ¶ Uncertain extraction must not silently become verified fact. Human decisions need durable actor and rationale information. Search has its own publication delay and permissions. Complete therefore means usable, traceable, permitted output, rather than merely a worker finishing without an exception.

Assume one million files/day averaging 2 MB, giving 2 TB/day raw input. Average arrival is about 11.6 files/s; a tenfold peak is about 116/s. ¶ At an illustrative five CPU-seconds/file, extraction consumes five million CPU-seconds/day. Dividing by 3,600 gives about 1,389 CPU-hours. Classification, I/O, retries, and review add work or delay. Measure actual files and model behaviour; these are accounting assumptions rather than extraction throughput or accuracy results.

A prototype saves a file and extracts it within the upload request. Difficult files lengthen the request, process loss can erase progress, and human review has no natural durable waiting point. ¶ Separate resumable byte ingestion from durable stages. Keep immutable source versions and identified outputs so retries and corrections retain evidence about earlier results. This fixes a concrete lifecycle and recovery problem before more workers are introduced for capacity.

POST /documents creates an upload intent; completion verifies stored bytes. Status exposes stages, while field reads return versioned values and provenance. Review decisions, reprocessing, and tracked deletion have separate operations. ¶ Search and export expose only authorised published output. Distinguish deletion acceptance from full cleanup. Define which version reprocessing creates: a new pipeline must not silently reinterpret old output under the same identity.

A document binds tenant, source version, digest, and state. Upload sessions track bytes. Workflows name input and pipeline versions; attempts carry epochs. Artifacts carry kind, version, and object reference. Fields retain value, confidence, source region, and extractor identity. ¶ Review records identify decision, actor, and version. Track publication and deletion progress, preserving tenant and version scope in keys. Retain sufficient provenance under an explicit lifecycle rather than keeping every raw intermediate forever.

UploadService and ContentValidator check ingestion. WorkflowCoordinator owns stages. Versioned Classifier and Extractor adapters generate results; ProvenanceRecorder links evidence. ReviewPolicy selects review, ReviewService records decisions, and Publisher creates permitted projections. Reconciler repairs stuck or orphaned work. ¶ Workers receive limited capabilities and cannot change tenant because a message supplies another tenant field. Keep generation of model output separate from deciding whether it is valid, reviewed, and ready to publish.

Use immutable object storage, durable relational workflow metadata, task delivery, bounded isolated compute or model workers, review interfaces, and a search projection. Scoped storage carries bulk bytes; metadata controls progress. ¶ A document waiting hours for review must release its compute slot and database transaction. Durable workflow state survives without an attached process. Link sources, artifacts, decisions, and publication generations so a failure can be investigated and repaired across those components.

Verify upload identity and digest, create a stable workflow, classify and extract, then validate structure and meaning. Save evidence and either publish or request review. A review decision creates a new validated version and publication intent. ¶ Each stage completes conditionally under its current epoch. Reprocessing creates a new run over identified input while retaining earlier provenance. Deletion blocks inappropriate later publication. These persisted conditions protect local progress even when attempts repeat or an old worker resumes.

Thirty days at 2 TB/day requires 60 TB of input before copies. Five million CPU-seconds/day divided by 86,400 gives about 57.9 continuously busy cores. At 60% target utilisation, 57.9 / 0.6 gives about 96.5 cores before other work and failure margin. ¶ A tenfold arrival peak exceeds the average arrival rate. Its duration, queue age, scaling delay, and admission determine whether objectives hold. Include large-file and model-memory distributions rather than treating every document as average-sized.

Upload transfer and durable acceptance have one timeline. Automated work adds queueing, classification, extraction, validation, and publication; human review creates a separate wait. ¶ Suppose queueing takes two seconds, classification one, extraction five, and validation/publication one. The automated path totals nine seconds in this model. These are assumptions to test. Report current stage and age so users can distinguish computation from a pending human decision.

Incomplete uploads, hostile or corrupt files, and crashes after artifact write need different repairs. Repeated tasks, stale attempts, invalid output, concurrent reviews, and missing provenance can each damage another boundary. ¶ Use stable artifact identity and conditional transitions. Quarantine unsupported output with a reason, and test deletion during processing. A finished label with missing, forbidden, or unexplained output still fails the business requirement even when workers report no exception.

Transfer bulk bytes directly through storage and scale independent extraction with bounded workers. Partition metadata by a justified tenant or workflow key, accounting for hot tenants and large files. ¶ Test batching and model-memory limits explicitly. More workers can saturate storage or an external model. Reserve separate replay and backfill capacity so upgrades do not starve live inputs or deletion. Expand the stage limiting useful output rather than multiplying every worker equally.

Source versions are immutable. Related workflow and review changes need suitable atomic transitions. Artifacts identify their input and transform; repeats use stable output identity and current-epoch completion. ¶ Search may lag publication while preserving access rules. External model calls can be uncertain or nondeterministic. Retain relevant requests and version information, validate results, and state the actual reproducibility boundary. A recorded model name alone does not establish that a later run produces the same answer.

Enforce tenant scope on objects, tasks, review, search, export, and audit. Bound worker resources and egress, validate supported files, and keep private data and credentials out of routine telemetry. ¶ Treat extracted text as untrusted content rather than infrastructure instructions. Reviewers need scoped access and traceable decisions. Deletion covers sensitive derived output as well as input bytes. Follow permission through every stage that reads, transforms, or republishes the document.

Version pipelines and schemas compatibly. Evaluate models on authorised samples or controlled cohorts before replacing output, and retain a usable earlier generation until the transition is verified. Drain workers through durable attempts. ¶ Restore exercises must reconnect objects, workflow state, decisions, and deletion history. Metadata-only restoration can leave missing artifacts or revive obsolete publication. Test the recovered journey before treating the platform as usable again.

Measure accepted bytes, stage waits, compute, retries, stale-completion rejection, invalid output, review age, provenance completeness, publication lag, tenant denial, and deletion progress. ¶ Confidence is not measured accuracy. Use an appropriate labelled evaluation for quality claims. Link source, run, attempt, model, artifact, and reviewer through privacy-safe evidence. This reveals whether a particular result is usable and where it stopped, beyond showing that workers are busy.

Include source and derived storage, compute or model calls, retries, review labour, search, transfer, telemetry, and recovery. Five million CPU-seconds/day describes compute; review may dominate operating cost. ¶ Use explicit unit rates and a stated review fraction. Avoid invented accuracy or throughput figures. Compare equal provenance, access, quality, and recovery requirements, since removing review or history may lower cost while changing the output users require.

Synchronous extraction fits a tiny demonstration while coupling upload to model delay and failure. Durable stages add metadata and operations but support recovery and human waits. Batching may improve resource use while adding delay and memory demand. ¶ Managed models or workflows delegate selected duties without automatically providing validation, provenance, tenant isolation, or review policy. Compare the complete output and recovery contract. Choose the smallest arrangement that supports those needs for the intended workload.

Start with immutable upload and explicit status. Add durable stages and stable artifacts before increasing concurrency. Give provenance and human decisions identities from the beginning. ¶ Then grow storage and workers independently and introduce authorised search and versioned reprocessing. Regional placement needs a named locality or recovery requirement and compatible lifecycle rules. Earlier results must stay explainable, and old work must continue to respect current access and deletion decisions.

Check whether a stage can silently change its input version, whether a superseded attempt can publish after deletion, and how reprocessing preserves human decisions. ¶ Test task repeats, artifact-write interruption, invalid output, concurrent reviews, stale completion, missing objects, index lag, and full restoration. A few correct extracted fields cannot establish recoverable progress and provenance. Review the connected lifecycle, including rejection, review, and deletion outcomes.

Implement three stages with immutable input identity, result version, and attempt epoch. Stop after writing an artifact but before recording completion. Replay it without duplicating the established output. ¶ Add a review state that releases compute while waiting. Pause an old worker, delete the document, then submit its completion. Publication must stay blocked by the authoritative current-state condition. Explain how that persisted check prevents stale work from reversing deletion, independently of whether cancellation reached the worker.''',
['Verified immutable upload',
 'Durable versioned extraction workflow',
 'Validation and human review',
 'Authorized publication with provenance'],
['Document owns source versions',
 'Workflow owns stage attempts',
 'Field links source region and model',
 'Review and deletion are durable state'],
'def complete_stage(run, epoch, artifact):\n    require(artifact.input_version == run.input_version)\n    validate_output_schema_and_provenance(artifact)\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_workflow(run.id)\n        require(current.epoch == epoch and not current.deleted)\n        insert_artifact_once(run.id, artifact.version, artifact)\n        advance_or_request_review(current, artifact)\n        insert_next_stage_outbox_if_needed(current)\n# Retry reuses identities; old attempts cannot publish new state.')

